1
00:00:04,667 --> 00:00:07,769
Politics is about using power
to distribute resources...

2
00:00:08,104 --> 00:00:10,706
and balance the different interests
of people.

3
00:00:11,774 --> 00:00:13,975
The Community: Tensions Under the Roof...

4
00:00:14,410 --> 00:00:17,649
is a social experiment
and a true political survival game...

5
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:21,583
where 12 men and women
with different beliefs come together.

6
00:00:22,318 --> 00:00:25,120
A week before moving in,

7
00:00:25,121 --> 00:00:28,690
all participants took a test
about social and political topics.

8
00:00:29,125 --> 00:00:32,127
(Discrimination against Black people
in America is somewhat justified.)

9
00:00:32,128 --> 00:00:35,196
Would anyone agree with this?

10
00:00:35,298 --> 00:00:37,766
(The lack of female roles in movies is
a serious problem.)

11
00:00:37,767 --> 00:00:40,035
(Speculative property buying
should be regulated.)

12
00:00:40,036 --> 00:00:42,237
I find it fascinating...

13
00:00:42,238 --> 00:00:45,073
that some people think this way.

14
00:00:45,074 --> 00:00:46,908
What do you mean by "PC people?"

15
00:00:46,909 --> 00:00:47,976
Correctness?

16
00:00:48,745 --> 00:00:52,481
That kind of thing is why Disney
and Marvel have gone downhill.

17
00:00:52,482 --> 00:00:54,817
"When women complain about mental health
issues like depression,"

18
00:00:54,818 --> 00:00:56,251
"they're often just being dramatic."

19
00:00:56,252 --> 00:00:57,720
I kind of agree.

20
00:00:57,721 --> 00:00:59,455
If they're complaining,
they're exaggerating.

21
00:00:59,656 --> 00:01:02,257
(Competition generally makes
the world better.)

22
00:01:02,258 --> 00:01:03,625
"I'd love to live in a country..."

23
00:01:03,626 --> 00:01:05,695
"where everyone earns..."

24
00:01:05,696 --> 00:01:06,928
"the same salary." I'd love that.

25
00:01:06,929 --> 00:01:07,996
This is easy.

26
00:01:08,264 --> 00:01:12,067
This test measures participants
across four scales.

27
00:01:12,201 --> 00:01:15,837
It consists of politics, gender, class,

28
00:01:15,838 --> 00:01:18,206
and openness.

29
00:01:18,624 --> 00:01:20,575
What if I don't get to say a word?

30
00:01:20,856 --> 00:01:22,311
This is no joke.

31
00:01:22,312 --> 00:01:24,112
Is this some kind of feminist test?

32
00:01:24,447 --> 00:01:28,016
The Politics section measures their
attitudes toward the role of government.

33
00:01:28,652 --> 00:01:30,152
The more they support
an expanded government role...

34
00:01:30,177 --> 00:01:32,278
in welfare and redistribution,
the more left-leaning they are.

35
00:01:32,755 --> 00:01:35,457
If the gap between people
who inherit a lot of wealth...

36
00:01:35,458 --> 00:01:38,827
and people from regular families
gets too big,

37
00:01:38,828 --> 00:01:41,363
the government should address it...

38
00:01:41,364 --> 00:01:43,765
through policies and social programs.

39
00:01:43,766 --> 00:01:46,501
I think it's right to collect taxes.

40
00:01:46,502 --> 00:01:48,070
I'd even be okay with higher taxes.

41
00:01:48,071 --> 00:01:50,939
The more they support
a smaller government,

42
00:01:50,940 --> 00:01:52,274
the more right-leaning they are.

43
00:01:52,275 --> 00:01:54,342
If you argue that society is unequal...

44
00:01:54,343 --> 00:01:56,077
because everyone starts
from a different place,

45
00:01:56,078 --> 00:01:59,981
when Generation A lives out its life
and dies,

46
00:01:59,982 --> 00:02:02,917
everyone would have to give all the money
they've earned to the government.

47
00:02:02,918 --> 00:02:04,352
Then the next generation would...

48
00:02:04,353 --> 00:02:07,155
start over from nothing.

49
00:02:07,156 --> 00:02:09,023
That would be the logic,

50
00:02:09,024 --> 00:02:10,525
which is ridiculous.

51
00:02:10,526 --> 00:02:12,728
Minimum wage is a way...

52
00:02:12,729 --> 00:02:13,995
to keep people in line.

53
00:02:13,996 --> 00:02:15,897
Why do people care so much
about the minimum wage?

54
00:02:15,898 --> 00:02:19,167
Because they want to get the minimum
without having to work harder.

55
00:02:19,168 --> 00:02:22,537
The gender section measures
attitudes toward feminism in general.

56
00:02:23,238 --> 00:02:25,641
The more they agree that men still have
significant advantages in modern society,

57
00:02:25,642 --> 00:02:28,376
the more feminist they are.

58
00:02:28,910 --> 00:02:31,980
The more they disagree,
the more they lean toward equalism.

59
00:02:32,382 --> 00:02:35,150
We're exposed to crimes targeting women,

60
00:02:35,151 --> 00:02:36,919
and you realize it's not something
that happens only to you.

61
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:39,555
You realize other women go through it too.

62
00:02:39,556 --> 00:02:40,756
Life is easier as a man.

63
00:02:40,957 --> 00:02:43,592
Of course, being a woman in Korea is...

64
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:46,995
harder than being a man.

65
00:02:46,996 --> 00:02:49,097
There are some types of women...

66
00:02:49,098 --> 00:02:51,166
I don't really like.

67
00:02:51,167 --> 00:02:54,436
They want their rights respected,

68
00:02:54,437 --> 00:02:57,372
but they also expect a lot from men.

69
00:02:57,607 --> 00:03:00,442
Being a woman in our society comes...

70
00:03:00,443 --> 00:03:02,010
with some advantages.

71
00:03:02,011 --> 00:03:04,346
Men have to serve in the military,

72
00:03:04,347 --> 00:03:06,782
but women don't.

73
00:03:06,783 --> 00:03:10,285
"Class" measures economic background
and views on class.

74
00:03:10,719 --> 00:03:14,589
If they grew up in a low-income family,
they lean more toward working class.

75
00:03:15,191 --> 00:03:18,727
The more affluent their family,
the more they lean upper-middle class.

76
00:03:18,728 --> 00:03:21,229
Both of my parents are professors.

77
00:03:21,230 --> 00:03:23,832
I learned art and Chinese.

78
00:03:23,833 --> 00:03:26,635
I had private tutors and went to academies
for almost everything.

79
00:03:26,636 --> 00:03:29,805
In my family, anything other than food
and basic living expenses...

80
00:03:29,806 --> 00:03:31,206
was considered a luxury.

81
00:03:31,207 --> 00:03:33,809
I studied in the UK in high school,

82
00:03:33,810 --> 00:03:35,377
and I went to university in Hong Kong.

83
00:03:35,378 --> 00:03:37,045
When I was young,

84
00:03:37,046 --> 00:03:39,314
my family of six lived
in a single-room apartment.

85
00:03:39,315 --> 00:03:41,149
We always had rats in our house.

86
00:03:41,150 --> 00:03:43,919
"Openness" measures how people feel...

87
00:03:43,920 --> 00:03:46,154
about traditional values
and minority groups.

88
00:03:46,589 --> 00:03:48,464
The more they accept
new moral standards and minorities,

89
00:03:48,465 --> 00:03:50,692
the more open-minded they are.

90
00:03:51,327 --> 00:03:54,629
The more they stick to traditional values
and majority norms,

91
00:03:54,630 --> 00:03:56,464
the more traditional they are.

92
00:03:56,465 --> 00:03:58,566
Same-sex couples and adoption...

93
00:03:58,567 --> 00:04:02,370
They should be given more rights.

94
00:04:02,371 --> 00:04:04,639
Homosexuality...

95
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:06,542
goes against the natural harmony...

96
00:04:06,543 --> 00:04:07,943
between men and women and yin and yang.

97
00:04:07,944 --> 00:04:11,746
Same-sex couples demanding
exactly the same marriage rights is...

98
00:04:12,048 --> 00:04:14,816
taking things a little too far.

99
00:04:14,817 --> 00:04:17,552
"Their protest blocked people
with disabilities from getting to work."

100
00:04:17,553 --> 00:04:20,122
"That's not right."

101
00:04:20,123 --> 00:04:23,125
But it makes me think, "How frustrated
must the protesters have been?"

102
00:04:23,126 --> 00:04:26,028
When you offer vegan options,

103
00:04:26,029 --> 00:04:27,462
vegetarians come...

104
00:04:27,797 --> 00:04:29,332
and ask if it's truly vegan.

105
00:04:29,333 --> 00:04:31,666
"Is it soy sauce?
Does it contain anything from animals?"

106
00:04:31,667 --> 00:04:33,769
"Did you use soy milk?
I can't drink milk."

107
00:04:33,770 --> 00:04:35,871
It just becomes a headache.

108
00:04:36,405 --> 00:04:38,373
The stronger their views are,

109
00:04:38,374 --> 00:04:40,408
the more vivid the color becomes.

110
00:04:40,610 --> 00:04:43,712
The more passive their views are,
the fainter the color is.

111
00:04:44,314 --> 00:04:46,848
Over the next nine days,
the contestants with different scores...

112
00:04:47,049 --> 00:04:50,184
will take part in a game
with the following rules.

113
00:04:50,486 --> 00:04:55,090
One, on The Community:
Tensions Under the Roof,

114
00:04:55,091 --> 00:04:58,427
they can build up prize money worth
up to 200,000 dollars,

115
00:04:58,428 --> 00:05:01,496
and the money will be divided
among the contestants.

116
00:05:01,799 --> 00:05:05,733
Two, the person who survives
until the end...

117
00:05:05,734 --> 00:05:08,370
can take home the prize money
they have earned.

118
00:05:08,371 --> 00:05:11,539
Multiple people can make it to the end.

119
00:05:12,008 --> 00:05:15,444
Three, the leader chosen by popular vote
has a major influence...

120
00:05:15,445 --> 00:05:18,947
on how the prize money is distributed
and who gets eliminated.

121
00:05:19,649 --> 00:05:24,019
Four, to survive until the end
and take home more money,

122
00:05:24,353 --> 00:05:27,455
contestants will need to become
leaders themselves...

123
00:05:27,756 --> 00:05:30,225
or get someone on their side elected
as a leader.

124
00:05:31,068 --> 00:05:34,737
This is why The Community:
Tensions Under the Roof is...

125
00:05:34,964 --> 00:05:37,699
a true political survival game.

126
00:05:37,967 --> 00:05:39,301
Everything can become
a source of conflict.

127
00:05:39,302 --> 00:05:41,370
We're building a country together.

128
00:05:41,371 --> 00:05:44,372
A safety net so that none of you will
have to give up on each other.

129
00:05:44,373 --> 00:05:46,374
If we were going to do this
based on compassion or good feelings,

130
00:05:46,375 --> 00:05:48,410
why are we doing a survival game?

131
00:05:48,411 --> 00:05:50,512
We only need one more vote
to register as a candidate.

132
00:05:50,513 --> 00:05:52,280
Let's convince two people just in case.

133
00:05:52,281 --> 00:05:54,316
Okay, I'm on it.

134
00:05:54,317 --> 00:05:57,752
But there's always a troublemaker...

135
00:05:57,753 --> 00:05:59,788
who threatens to divide a community.

136
00:05:59,789 --> 00:06:02,057
If we publicly put the troublemaker
on trial...

137
00:06:02,058 --> 00:06:03,891
That's not fair.

138
00:06:03,892 --> 00:06:07,194
How can you talk about community values
with someone who's lying to everyone?

139
00:06:07,195 --> 00:06:09,230
Even here, elites are starting to emerge.

140
00:06:09,231 --> 00:06:11,299
They might form an alliance and attack us.

141
00:06:11,300 --> 00:06:12,634
When we were picking the leader,

142
00:06:12,635 --> 00:06:14,603
didn't all the women vote for women?

143
00:06:15,338 --> 00:06:16,972
A hidden disruptor,

144
00:06:17,373 --> 00:06:20,408
secretly working to tear everyone apart
and fuel conflict.

145
00:06:20,877 --> 00:06:23,378
What is the mole's ultimate goal?

146
00:06:23,847 --> 00:06:26,148
And who are they?

147
00:06:26,649 --> 00:06:30,118
When the residents find out
about the mole,

148
00:06:30,119 --> 00:06:32,587
what kind of community will they build?

149
00:06:33,923 --> 00:06:35,323
The true political survival game...

150
00:06:35,525 --> 00:06:38,493
begins now.

151
00:06:39,996 --> 00:06:41,363
(Tensions Under the Roof)

152
00:06:41,364 --> 00:06:43,632
(The Community)

153
00:06:43,633 --> 00:06:49,638
(The Beginning of a Community,
The Beginning of Politics)

154
00:06:50,597 --> 00:06:54,466
(Ted, YouTuber: right, feminism,
upper-middle, open-minded)

155
00:06:57,647 --> 00:06:59,014
This is legit.

156
00:07:04,153 --> 00:07:06,254
This is completely different
from what I imagined.

157
00:07:06,756 --> 00:07:08,290
My real name is Lee Seung Kook.

158
00:07:08,291 --> 00:07:12,293
I'm a YouTuber, but I also do some TV.

159
00:07:12,294 --> 00:07:17,265
I'd say
I'm a professional attention seeker.

160
00:07:17,266 --> 00:07:18,967
I've never gone by this name before.

161
00:07:18,968 --> 00:07:19,934
It's Ted.

162
00:07:19,935 --> 00:07:22,003
Ted is the main character...

163
00:07:22,004 --> 00:07:24,072
in a drama I've been really into lately.

164
00:07:24,073 --> 00:07:26,842
Ted is a YouTuber
who does all kinds of activities.

165
00:07:27,476 --> 00:07:29,310
He has a mixed profile.

166
00:07:29,311 --> 00:07:31,712
He's open-minded
about feminism and minority groups.

167
00:07:31,713 --> 00:07:33,815
(Ted: Right, feminism,
upper-middle, open-minded)

168
00:07:41,524 --> 00:07:43,792
(Everyone has their own chat room,
and they can visit each other.)

169
00:07:43,793 --> 00:07:44,860
Is this the right room?

170
00:07:47,229 --> 00:07:48,563
I came in, and it led here.

171
00:07:51,066 --> 00:07:51,967
Am I in the wrong room?

172
00:07:57,873 --> 00:08:00,608
It's different from the chat room.

173
00:08:01,544 --> 00:08:02,644
(Ted)

174
00:08:02,645 --> 00:08:03,878
Number 11.

175
00:08:06,015 --> 00:08:07,816
(Residents can only enter
their own bedrooms on the second floor.)

176
00:08:07,817 --> 00:08:10,252
(They can't visit each other.)

177
00:08:11,954 --> 00:08:13,054
What is this?

178
00:08:15,358 --> 00:08:17,425
I'm not as neutral as I thought.

179
00:08:17,426 --> 00:08:19,026
(Contestants could be eliminated
if their score is exposed.)

180
00:08:19,027 --> 00:08:20,628
(They can use fake identities
to hide their true beliefs.)

181
00:08:20,629 --> 00:08:21,496
Whatever.

182
00:08:21,931 --> 00:08:22,797
There's a foolproof strategy.

183
00:08:22,798 --> 00:08:24,399
Let's do everything we can...

184
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:26,601
before things completely fall apart.

185
00:08:26,602 --> 00:08:28,270
Then you shouldn't be the one leading
the meeting.

186
00:08:28,271 --> 00:08:29,738
You're just doing what's best
for your own survival.

187
00:08:29,739 --> 00:08:31,907
Let's survive another day!

188
00:08:31,908 --> 00:08:32,941
(Ted, the community peacemaker)

189
00:08:34,410 --> 00:08:37,946
(Michael, rapper: left, equalism,
upper-middle, conservative)

190
00:08:37,947 --> 00:08:39,447
Hello, I'm YunB.

191
00:08:39,615 --> 00:08:42,717
I've been on a lot of survival shows.

192
00:08:42,718 --> 00:08:44,552
I don't know if it's normal,

193
00:08:44,720 --> 00:08:46,822
but I think I'm addicted to them,

194
00:08:46,823 --> 00:08:48,189
if I'm being honest.

195
00:08:48,357 --> 00:08:49,924
For me, survival shows are...

196
00:08:49,925 --> 00:08:51,826
a way to meet new people.

197
00:08:51,827 --> 00:08:53,861
I just find them so much fun.

198
00:08:53,996 --> 00:08:56,130
My baptismal name is Michael,

199
00:08:56,131 --> 00:08:58,232
and it was also my English name...

200
00:08:58,233 --> 00:08:59,667
until high school.

201
00:08:59,770 --> 00:09:02,871
Michael is a businessman and rapper
who graduated from NYU.

202
00:09:03,506 --> 00:09:07,442
He's upper-middle class
and conservative in every way.

203
00:09:07,443 --> 00:09:09,644
(Michael: Left, equalism,
upper-middle, conservative)

204
00:09:10,045 --> 00:09:11,212
We have to attack.

205
00:09:11,213 --> 00:09:12,914
That's nonsense.

206
00:09:12,915 --> 00:09:15,016
I'll kill the ones with more money.

207
00:09:15,017 --> 00:09:16,551
Just for the two of you.

208
00:09:16,552 --> 00:09:19,688
I hate pretending to be a good person.

209
00:09:19,689 --> 00:09:21,489
Shall we take out the easy target?

210
00:09:21,791 --> 00:09:24,793
Who can I convince?

211
00:09:24,994 --> 00:09:28,163
(Michael, a veteran player)

212
00:09:30,232 --> 00:09:31,933
(Hippo, writer: left, feminism,
working class, open-minded)

213
00:09:31,934 --> 00:09:36,904
I'm a writer under the pen name Ha Mina.

214
00:09:37,173 --> 00:09:38,439
My first book was...

215
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:42,410
"Crazy, Weird, Arrogant, Brilliant Women."

216
00:09:42,511 --> 00:09:45,180
It's about women's depression.

217
00:09:45,181 --> 00:09:48,716
After the 2016 Gangnam Station murder,

218
00:09:48,785 --> 00:09:51,986
there were a lot of movements
among young women.

219
00:09:51,987 --> 00:09:54,589
I worked as a women's rights activist
for three years.

220
00:09:54,890 --> 00:09:56,524
I want to be like a hippo...

221
00:09:56,525 --> 00:09:59,895
because it's a herbivore,
but fierce and tough.

222
00:09:59,896 --> 00:10:01,929
That's why I chose that nickname.

223
00:10:02,198 --> 00:10:04,666
If anyone messes with the little hippo,

224
00:10:04,667 --> 00:10:05,800
they're going to be in big trouble.

225
00:10:06,969 --> 00:10:10,004
Hippo is a nonfiction writer who graduated
from Seoul National University.

226
00:10:10,207 --> 00:10:12,641
She has the highest overall score
among the residents...

227
00:10:12,642 --> 00:10:15,176
and has a background
as a feminist activist.

228
00:10:15,177 --> 00:10:16,945
(Hippo: left, feminism,
working class, open-minded)

229
00:10:16,946 --> 00:10:18,847
"They're calling me a feminist again."

230
00:10:19,148 --> 00:10:22,484
The fact that people in Korea
keep calling me a feminist...

231
00:10:22,485 --> 00:10:24,152
shows just how strong the stigma...

232
00:10:24,153 --> 00:10:26,187
around feminism is here.

233
00:10:26,188 --> 00:10:29,157
I want to show...

234
00:10:29,158 --> 00:10:32,293
that even if they call me a feminist,

235
00:10:32,294 --> 00:10:33,828
I'm not going to let myself be put
in that box.

236
00:10:33,829 --> 00:10:37,198
That's why I felt like I had to do this.

237
00:10:40,236 --> 00:10:41,898
(Sugar, men's magazine model)

238
00:10:41,899 --> 00:10:43,736
(Right, equalism,
upper-middle, open-minded)

239
00:10:43,737 --> 00:10:46,140
Hello, my name is Kim Na Jung.

240
00:10:46,141 --> 00:10:47,875
I'm a freelance anchor...

241
00:10:47,876 --> 00:10:52,414
and I also model for a men's magazine
called Maxim.

242
00:10:52,415 --> 00:10:54,316
Sugar went to Ewha Womans University.

243
00:10:54,317 --> 00:10:57,452
She's a model who won
a Maxim modeling competition.

244
00:10:57,720 --> 00:10:59,420
("I don't get feminism"
After Reading "Kim Ji Young, Born 1982.")

245
00:10:59,421 --> 00:11:01,580
She went viral
for her comments about equalism.

246
00:11:01,924 --> 00:11:03,424
She's upper-middle class.

247
00:11:03,425 --> 00:11:05,093
(Upper-middle)

248
00:11:05,094 --> 00:11:07,595
If I'm being honest,

249
00:11:07,596 --> 00:11:10,064
I didn't experience...

250
00:11:10,065 --> 00:11:14,068
much discrimination growing up.

251
00:11:14,269 --> 00:11:17,372
So I feel like I'm living
in a much better time now.

252
00:11:17,706 --> 00:11:20,908
The person I admire most is
Marilyn Monroe.

253
00:11:20,909 --> 00:11:23,411
I decided to become a model...

254
00:11:23,412 --> 00:11:26,848
because I wanted to challenge myself...

255
00:11:26,849 --> 00:11:29,417
to become a sexy icon like her.

256
00:11:33,122 --> 00:11:34,890
(Superman, People Power Party)

257
00:11:34,891 --> 00:11:36,624
(Right, equalism,
upper-middle, conservative)

258
00:11:38,260 --> 00:11:41,562
I'm a politician
in the conservative People Power Party.

259
00:11:41,730 --> 00:11:43,498
My name is Kim Jae Sub.

260
00:11:43,599 --> 00:11:46,167
Superman has hair like mine,

261
00:11:46,301 --> 00:11:47,501
and he has a strong jawline like me too.

262
00:11:47,637 --> 00:11:50,070
I thought it suited me.

263
00:11:50,071 --> 00:11:52,907
Superman is a politician
with the People Power Party.

264
00:11:53,375 --> 00:11:55,142
He consistently scores...

265
00:11:55,143 --> 00:11:56,685
on the conservative side
across all scales.

266
00:11:56,686 --> 00:11:58,136
(Superman: right, equalism,
upper-middle, conservative)

267
00:12:00,249 --> 00:12:03,017
My family has a strong "red complex."

268
00:12:03,018 --> 00:12:05,919
One thing I absolutely won't compromise
on is our relationship with North Korea.

269
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:06,988
I come from a family of veterans.

270
00:12:07,155 --> 00:12:08,790
Some members
of my paternal grandfather's family...

271
00:12:08,791 --> 00:12:10,925
died during the Korean War.

272
00:12:10,926 --> 00:12:13,094
They're all my cousins.

273
00:12:13,095 --> 00:12:16,263
Both sides of my family have veterans,

274
00:12:16,264 --> 00:12:18,065
so we're pretty strongly anti-communist.

275
00:12:20,236 --> 00:12:21,169
To the right?

276
00:12:21,170 --> 00:12:23,264
(Polar Bear, Democratic Party: left,
feminism, working class, open-minded)

277
00:12:23,265 --> 00:12:25,607
Hello, my name is Park Seong Min.

278
00:12:25,774 --> 00:12:28,008
I'm a politician.

279
00:12:29,144 --> 00:12:32,813
People say polar bears can tear
people apart.

280
00:12:32,814 --> 00:12:34,915
One of my close friends showed me
a polar bear and said,

281
00:12:34,916 --> 00:12:36,017
"That's so you."

282
00:12:36,117 --> 00:12:38,820
He thought I seemed nice on the outside,

283
00:12:38,821 --> 00:12:40,454
but I can be scary...

284
00:12:40,455 --> 00:12:41,890
when I get into a debate.

285
00:12:41,891 --> 00:12:45,526
I chose Polar Bear because of that.

286
00:12:45,661 --> 00:12:47,562
You can't regulate every situation
the same way...

287
00:12:47,563 --> 00:12:49,197
because every incident is different.

288
00:12:49,198 --> 00:12:51,633
You can't put every little detail
into the law.

289
00:12:51,634 --> 00:12:53,701
- But actually...
- It's the government's responsibility...

290
00:12:53,702 --> 00:12:56,204
to take action
and protect people's safety.

291
00:12:56,205 --> 00:12:58,072
Polar Bear served
as the youngest presidential secretary...

292
00:12:58,073 --> 00:13:00,575
under the Moon Jae In administration.

293
00:13:00,576 --> 00:13:02,510
She's a politician
with the Democratic Party of Korea.

294
00:13:02,511 --> 00:13:05,747
She scores clearly on the progressive side
across all four scales.

295
00:13:05,748 --> 00:13:07,649
(Polar Bear: Left, feminism,
working class, open-minded)

296
00:13:08,884 --> 00:13:10,151
It's easiest to respond...

297
00:13:10,152 --> 00:13:12,353
when someone says something...

298
00:13:12,354 --> 00:13:13,688
that's simply not true.

299
00:13:13,689 --> 00:13:15,422
If I've done my research, I can just say,

300
00:13:15,423 --> 00:13:18,126
"What you just said is wrong,"

301
00:13:18,127 --> 00:13:19,961
and that can easily turn
the whole debate...

302
00:13:19,962 --> 00:13:21,762
in my favor.

303
00:13:21,763 --> 00:13:24,798
When they bring up a topic...

304
00:13:24,799 --> 00:13:27,168
they really shouldn't be touching,

305
00:13:27,169 --> 00:13:29,136
I think, "You're done. You're finished."

306
00:13:29,137 --> 00:13:31,506
I just can't stand it when they go
after sensitive social issues...

307
00:13:31,507 --> 00:13:33,774
or vulnerable social groups.

308
00:13:34,176 --> 00:13:37,245
I want to prove here...

309
00:13:37,246 --> 00:13:38,813
that kindness can survive.

310
00:13:38,814 --> 00:13:40,281
If we start relying on goodwill,

311
00:13:40,282 --> 00:13:41,482
then it's not really a system anymore.

312
00:13:41,483 --> 00:13:44,051
What's the best way to keep ourselves
from becoming divided?

313
00:13:44,052 --> 00:13:46,254
I will make sure
traitors face consequences...

314
00:13:46,255 --> 00:13:47,221
one way or another.

315
00:13:47,222 --> 00:13:48,689
I want everyone to be safe...

316
00:13:48,690 --> 00:13:49,923
in this community.

317
00:13:49,924 --> 00:13:51,359
And that right there is hypocrisy.

318
00:13:51,360 --> 00:13:53,361
(Polar Bear
from the Democratic Party of Korea)

319
00:13:53,362 --> 00:13:54,962
(Superman from the People Power Party)

320
00:13:54,963 --> 00:13:55,864
The Democratic Party is just...

321
00:13:56,431 --> 00:13:58,499
That's why being progressive is always
so difficult.

322
00:14:01,537 --> 00:14:03,103
(Dark Knight,
former Special Forces soldier)

323
00:14:03,104 --> 00:14:04,873
(Right, equalism,
working class, conservative)

324
00:14:05,741 --> 00:14:08,709
Hello. I've been
out of the Special Forces...

325
00:14:08,710 --> 00:14:11,112
for five years now.

326
00:14:11,113 --> 00:14:12,747
My name is Lee Chang Jun.

327
00:14:12,748 --> 00:14:15,616
"Dark Knight" means a knight of darkness.

328
00:14:15,617 --> 00:14:17,919
I'm usually active at night.

329
00:14:18,220 --> 00:14:20,822
Dark Knight served in the Special Forces,

330
00:14:20,823 --> 00:14:23,424
the 707, for 13 years.

331
00:14:24,160 --> 00:14:25,860
He's strongly conservative,

332
00:14:25,861 --> 00:14:27,995
and he scored highest
on the working-class side.

333
00:14:29,131 --> 00:14:30,865
I didn't grow up with much financially.

334
00:14:30,932 --> 00:14:34,535
If you want to get out
of a tough situation,

335
00:14:34,770 --> 00:14:36,904
you really have to work for it.

336
00:14:37,140 --> 00:14:39,540
I believe that if you work extremely hard,

337
00:14:39,541 --> 00:14:41,376
you can get yourself out of any situation.

338
00:14:41,377 --> 00:14:42,410
Extremely hard.

339
00:14:43,879 --> 00:14:46,948
(Checking the emergency escape device
as soon as he enters his room)

340
00:14:48,617 --> 00:14:50,951
I grew up constantly reading the room,

341
00:14:50,952 --> 00:14:52,485
so I know I'm good at that.

342
00:14:52,486 --> 00:14:55,055
Adapting to my surroundings is...

343
00:14:55,056 --> 00:14:56,423
something I'm confident in.

344
00:14:56,692 --> 00:14:58,526
I never blame my circumstances.

345
00:14:58,894 --> 00:15:00,962
I'm good at accepting things as they are.

346
00:15:02,598 --> 00:15:03,965
The world we're living in here...

347
00:15:03,966 --> 00:15:06,167
is for elites.

348
00:15:06,168 --> 00:15:07,768
It's not a world for ordinary people.

349
00:15:07,769 --> 00:15:10,438
You guys are hanging out
in an upper-class society.

350
00:15:10,439 --> 00:15:11,538
How nice is that?

351
00:15:11,539 --> 00:15:13,439
You just move a pen,
and the money starts piling up.

352
00:15:13,440 --> 00:15:15,476
You sign some stuff
and just collect the money.

353
00:15:15,743 --> 00:15:19,480
(Dark Knight,
the outsider of the community)

354
00:15:21,049 --> 00:15:22,016
(Genie, elite office worker)

355
00:15:22,017 --> 00:15:23,684
(Right, feminism,
upper-middle, conservative)

356
00:15:23,685 --> 00:15:24,918
There's even a shop.

357
00:15:26,855 --> 00:15:28,789
Hello, my name is Lee Gina.

358
00:15:28,790 --> 00:15:31,725
I chose Genie
because of the genie from "Aladdin."

359
00:15:31,726 --> 00:15:33,761
Genie can grant other people's wishes.

360
00:15:33,762 --> 00:15:37,498
I'm not someone
who lives for other people, though.

361
00:15:37,966 --> 00:15:41,402
Genie is an elite who graduated
from the University of Hong Kong.

362
00:15:41,403 --> 00:15:43,105
She's conservative...

363
00:15:43,106 --> 00:15:45,006
and the only one
who scored three points...

364
00:15:45,007 --> 00:15:46,674
on the upper-middle end
of the class scale.

365
00:15:47,709 --> 00:15:52,279
Hong Kong may be in a bit of decline now,

366
00:15:52,280 --> 00:15:54,581
but back then, even students
who got perfect scores...

367
00:15:54,582 --> 00:15:57,384
on China's college entrance exam
would come to our school.

368
00:15:57,385 --> 00:16:00,254
Our school was ranked number one in Asia.

369
00:16:00,255 --> 00:16:02,723
I'm still proud of that.

370
00:16:02,791 --> 00:16:04,726
I skipped a year in high school...

371
00:16:04,727 --> 00:16:06,594
and graduated from university early.

372
00:16:06,595 --> 00:16:08,629
I landed my first job...

373
00:16:09,031 --> 00:16:10,898
when I was 21 through an open recruitment.

374
00:16:10,899 --> 00:16:12,700
I work in marketing now.

375
00:16:13,502 --> 00:16:15,436
Work as hard as you want.
It's still going to be mine.

376
00:16:15,637 --> 00:16:18,305
Dark Knight, do you think
I'm just putting on a show?

377
00:16:18,306 --> 00:16:19,273
No.

378
00:16:22,544 --> 00:16:25,146
I feel like you resent people...

379
00:16:25,147 --> 00:16:27,848
who grew up wealthy.

380
00:16:27,849 --> 00:16:29,884
"Money isn't what matters.
It's what's in your heart,"

381
00:16:29,885 --> 00:16:31,652
or "There are things
more important than money."

382
00:16:31,653 --> 00:16:34,555
But there are relationships you lose
when you don't have money.

383
00:16:34,556 --> 00:16:36,189
I'm suspicious of Genie.

384
00:16:36,190 --> 00:16:37,925
Genie is really something.

385
00:16:37,926 --> 00:16:40,361
She's the most ambitious.

386
00:16:40,829 --> 00:16:44,565
(Genie, the most honest desires)

387
00:16:52,875 --> 00:16:55,074
(Benjamin, lawyer)

388
00:16:55,075 --> 00:16:57,345
(Right, equalism,
working class, open-minded)

389
00:16:59,781 --> 00:17:01,882
My name is Lim Hyun Seo.

390
00:17:02,053 --> 00:17:05,686
I'm a lawyer. I'm pretty close
to what you'd call an elite.

391
00:17:05,687 --> 00:17:07,321
I got a good education,

392
00:17:07,355 --> 00:17:09,256
and I've been doing well
for myself because of it.

393
00:17:09,257 --> 00:17:11,658
I'm a breadwinner in my early 30s.

394
00:17:11,659 --> 00:17:14,695
Benjamin is a lawyer who graduated
from Seoul National University Law School.

395
00:17:14,696 --> 00:17:18,032
He's a centrist who scores low
across all four scales.

396
00:17:18,033 --> 00:17:19,199
(Right, equalism,
working class, open-minded)

397
00:17:20,001 --> 00:17:21,836
Gosh! "Benjamin."

398
00:17:24,507 --> 00:17:25,439
Good to see you.

399
00:17:26,808 --> 00:17:28,342
What's up, the Community?

400
00:17:28,343 --> 00:17:29,477
This is Benjamin.

401
00:17:29,478 --> 00:17:31,745
Benjamin Franklin is...

402
00:17:31,746 --> 00:17:33,746
on the 100-dollar bill,

403
00:17:33,747 --> 00:17:36,717
so "Benjamin" is slang for money.

404
00:17:36,985 --> 00:17:40,054
Since I'm a lawyer...

405
00:17:40,055 --> 00:17:42,323
and have a relatively stable life,

406
00:17:42,457 --> 00:17:43,591
it's pretty easy to guess...

407
00:17:43,792 --> 00:17:45,059
what my beliefs are.

408
00:17:45,561 --> 00:17:47,795
So I want to make sure
I'm not so predictable.

409
00:17:47,796 --> 00:17:51,266
Right now, my hair is unkempt,
and I'm wearing glasses,

410
00:17:51,566 --> 00:17:53,367
so I might come across as a little nerdy.

411
00:17:53,368 --> 00:17:55,069
I want to try
a completely different style...

412
00:17:55,214 --> 00:17:56,780
that has nothing to do with my life.

413
00:17:56,805 --> 00:17:59,340
I want to play a young man here.

414
00:17:59,341 --> 00:18:01,976
Like a carefree, immature liberal guy...

415
00:18:02,211 --> 00:18:05,379
who grew up in Guam.

416
00:18:05,380 --> 00:18:06,781
That's my plan.

417
00:18:06,782 --> 00:18:08,816
I left my name tag in my room.

418
00:18:08,817 --> 00:18:10,184
I'm Benjamin.

419
00:18:10,185 --> 00:18:12,153
The dual language transition is...

420
00:18:12,154 --> 00:18:13,489
Do you usually wear glasses?

421
00:18:13,490 --> 00:18:15,022
- No, I don't.
- Is it just part of the look?

422
00:18:15,023 --> 00:18:17,625
I wasn't allowed to speak Korean at home.

423
00:18:17,859 --> 00:18:20,427
Tonight, the show is mine!

424
00:18:21,129 --> 00:18:23,730
(Benjamin, the perfect disguise)

425
00:18:24,033 --> 00:18:25,466
(Nangja)

426
00:18:25,467 --> 00:18:27,534
(Nangja, an actress who used to work
as a bodyguard)

427
00:18:27,535 --> 00:18:29,470
(Right, equalism,
working class, conservative)

428
00:18:31,239 --> 00:18:32,873
Hello, my name is Lee Su Ryeon.

429
00:18:32,874 --> 00:18:35,042
I'm an actress,

430
00:18:35,043 --> 00:18:36,743
but I do a few other things on the side.

431
00:18:37,112 --> 00:18:39,780
My nickname is Nangja.

432
00:18:39,781 --> 00:18:41,782
It's kind of like "burangja,"

433
00:18:42,016 --> 00:18:44,685
a vagrant wandering the streets.

434
00:18:44,954 --> 00:18:48,189
It's also like the expression
"blood was spilled everywhere."

435
00:18:48,456 --> 00:18:49,924
Nangja is an actress...

436
00:18:49,925 --> 00:18:52,726
who was the first female bodyguard
at the Blue House.

437
00:18:53,161 --> 00:18:54,195
(Nangja)

438
00:18:54,196 --> 00:18:55,796
Even among conservatives,

439
00:18:55,797 --> 00:18:58,866
she strongly opposes feminism.

440
00:18:59,201 --> 00:19:02,203
(Which area do you think you'd clash
with someone over the most?)

441
00:19:02,514 --> 00:19:03,571
Gender.

442
00:19:03,771 --> 00:19:05,239
I went to all-girls schools my whole life.

443
00:19:05,240 --> 00:19:07,541
I also went through military training
as a bodyguard.

444
00:19:07,542 --> 00:19:09,910
After experiencing all of that,

445
00:19:09,911 --> 00:19:13,614
I'd probably be put off if someone
who hasn't experienced either side...

446
00:19:13,815 --> 00:19:16,217
tried to talk to me about gender.

447
00:19:16,752 --> 00:19:18,920
Smooth talk and lip service.

448
00:19:18,921 --> 00:19:20,088
Who doesn't know how to do that?

449
00:19:20,388 --> 00:19:22,223
They've been hypocritical this whole time.

450
00:19:22,224 --> 00:19:24,425
I need to make sure
I'm the one who survives.

451
00:19:24,793 --> 00:19:26,661
(Nangja, the one who takes the masks off)

452
00:19:29,197 --> 00:19:30,531
(Gray, big data specialist)

453
00:19:30,532 --> 00:19:32,101
(Left, feminism,
upper-middle, open-minded)

454
00:19:32,102 --> 00:19:33,602
My name is Jeon Min Ki.

455
00:19:33,603 --> 00:19:35,536
I chose Gray as my nickname...

456
00:19:35,537 --> 00:19:38,773
because I don't believe
everything is black and white.

457
00:19:38,774 --> 00:19:41,809
The world isn't just black or white.

458
00:19:41,810 --> 00:19:45,880
Everyone is a mix of black and white.
We're all gray.

459
00:19:45,881 --> 00:19:49,517
We're all different shades of gray.

460
00:19:49,785 --> 00:19:53,087
Gray is a big data expert
who used to be a news anchor.

461
00:19:54,056 --> 00:19:55,257
What makes him interesting is...

462
00:19:55,258 --> 00:19:57,425
that he's upper-middle class
but also strongly progressive.

463
00:19:57,426 --> 00:19:59,126
(Gray: left, feminism,
upper-middle, open-minded)

464
00:19:59,127 --> 00:20:00,762
How many votes have we secured?

465
00:20:00,763 --> 00:20:02,597
It's like building a military group.

466
00:20:02,664 --> 00:20:04,566
I'm still suspicious of Michael.

467
00:20:04,567 --> 00:20:06,634
They're trying to get everyone
to turn on them.

468
00:20:06,635 --> 00:20:09,103
They're all lying. It's so annoying. Gosh!

469
00:20:09,104 --> 00:20:10,971
You can't trust anyone's word.

470
00:20:10,972 --> 00:20:13,040
You have to believe what they show you.

471
00:20:13,074 --> 00:20:16,610
(Gray, the political kingmaker)

472
00:20:18,613 --> 00:20:19,846
(Go Ae Shin, ice hockey coach)

473
00:20:19,847 --> 00:20:21,882
My name is An Geun Yeong.

474
00:20:22,083 --> 00:20:24,952
I've played ice hockey since I was a kid.

475
00:20:24,953 --> 00:20:27,988
I'm retired now and work as a coach.

476
00:20:28,157 --> 00:20:29,890
My nickname is Go Ae Shin.

477
00:20:29,891 --> 00:20:31,792
She's the main character...

478
00:20:31,793 --> 00:20:34,661
in one of my favorite dramas,
"Mr. Sunshine."

479
00:20:34,662 --> 00:20:37,598
I thought it was really cool...

480
00:20:37,599 --> 00:20:39,933
that a woman in that era...

481
00:20:39,934 --> 00:20:41,802
could be so patriotic.

482
00:20:41,803 --> 00:20:45,806
Go Ae Shin is
the only female ice hockey coach in Korea.

483
00:20:45,807 --> 00:20:47,674
She's pretty moderate politically,

484
00:20:47,675 --> 00:20:50,744
but she's conservative because she has
a lot of experience in group settings.

485
00:20:50,745 --> 00:20:52,079
(Left, feminism,
upper-middle, conservative)

486
00:20:52,080 --> 00:20:56,850
I find it hard to understand people
who are very individualistic.

487
00:20:57,086 --> 00:20:59,386
I grew up
in conservative group environments...

488
00:20:59,387 --> 00:21:01,355
since I was young.

489
00:21:01,356 --> 00:21:04,224
Because of the culture I grew up in,

490
00:21:04,225 --> 00:21:06,460
it's kind of shocking...

491
00:21:06,461 --> 00:21:08,129
to see someone skip a team dinner...

492
00:21:08,130 --> 00:21:11,399
just because they're not feeling well...

493
00:21:11,400 --> 00:21:13,000
or they're in a bad mood.

494
00:21:13,001 --> 00:21:16,270
When you're part of a group,

495
00:21:16,271 --> 00:21:19,440
there are certain boundaries
you should respect.

496
00:21:19,441 --> 00:21:20,508
I'm a young boomer, maybe.

497
00:21:21,310 --> 00:21:24,211
I'm a little conservative in that sense.

498
00:21:24,579 --> 00:21:27,314
(All 12 people have checked in.)

499
00:21:28,750 --> 00:21:29,550
What is this?

500
00:21:30,219 --> 00:21:31,385
Why is this already fun?

501
00:21:35,824 --> 00:21:38,626
This is an announcement
from the Community Center.

502
00:21:38,627 --> 00:21:40,561
- Finally!
- All residents,

503
00:21:40,796 --> 00:21:43,064
please gather in the main hall.

504
00:21:43,399 --> 00:21:44,665
The main hall.

505
00:21:46,802 --> 00:21:50,470
I'll bring a notebook with me.

506
00:21:50,839 --> 00:21:53,808
I might feel awkward if I'm early,
so let me take my time.

507
00:21:54,543 --> 00:21:58,813
(Benjamin, Polar Bear)

508
00:22:00,784 --> 00:22:02,016
Is this the main hall?

509
00:22:07,422 --> 00:22:09,390
- Hello.
- Hello.

510
00:22:10,158 --> 00:22:13,227
- This is the main hall, right?
- I think so.

511
00:22:17,499 --> 00:22:18,732
- Hello.
- Hello.

512
00:22:19,068 --> 00:22:20,401
- Hello.
- Hello.

513
00:22:20,402 --> 00:22:21,302
Hello.

514
00:22:23,872 --> 00:22:25,506
- Hello.
- Hello.

515
00:22:25,507 --> 00:22:26,874
Is this the main hall?

516
00:22:28,210 --> 00:22:29,743
- Hello.
- Hello.

517
00:22:29,744 --> 00:22:31,044
- Hello.
- Hello.

518
00:22:31,613 --> 00:22:32,613
Hello.

519
00:22:34,549 --> 00:22:36,617
Your room is the closest,
but you're the last one to show up.

520
00:22:36,618 --> 00:22:38,785
- Am I late?
- Oh, that's not your room.

521
00:22:39,921 --> 00:22:40,854
I'm sorry.

522
00:22:40,990 --> 00:22:41,856
That's okay.

523
00:22:42,257 --> 00:22:43,424
I thought that was your room.

524
00:22:46,294 --> 00:22:47,728
I feel like...

525
00:22:48,964 --> 00:22:50,898
Anyway, we're all here.

526
00:22:52,334 --> 00:22:53,367
I'm so nervous.

527
00:22:55,304 --> 00:22:56,370
My heart is racing.

528
00:22:56,371 --> 00:22:59,606
I can't really imagine
what this'll be like.

529
00:22:59,607 --> 00:23:00,473
I know.

530
00:23:02,644 --> 00:23:04,712
Your nickname sounds like a real name.

531
00:23:05,213 --> 00:23:06,647
(Go Ae Shin sounds like a real name.)

532
00:23:06,648 --> 00:23:08,115
I'm curious.

533
00:23:08,250 --> 00:23:09,250
"Nangja."

534
00:23:10,785 --> 00:23:12,219
"Dark Knight."

535
00:23:12,320 --> 00:23:14,155
- "Dark Knight."
- Yes.

536
00:23:14,491 --> 00:23:16,223
They asked us...

537
00:23:16,224 --> 00:23:18,225
to use fake names,

538
00:23:18,226 --> 00:23:21,662
so I'm not sure
how much we should be saying here.

539
00:23:21,663 --> 00:23:23,864
I think we can at least say
how old we are.

540
00:23:24,266 --> 00:23:25,265
Is that so?

541
00:23:25,266 --> 00:23:27,866
(All residents have belief scores.)

542
00:23:27,867 --> 00:23:30,737
(If their belief scores are exposed,
they could be eliminated.)

543
00:23:31,140 --> 00:23:33,140
- Aren't you hungry?
- I'm so hungry.

544
00:23:34,009 --> 00:23:35,709
When was your last meal?

545
00:23:35,977 --> 00:23:37,711
I had no idea I'd be starving like this.

546
00:23:37,712 --> 00:23:40,248
- I think we can just eat this.
- I think so.

547
00:23:40,249 --> 00:23:41,381
Do you want me to pass some over?

548
00:23:41,382 --> 00:23:43,885
This is 500ml.

549
00:23:44,420 --> 00:23:46,187
If it's too much, we can divvy it up.

550
00:23:46,188 --> 00:23:47,055
Anyone?

551
00:23:47,056 --> 00:23:49,256
If you're Superman,
you should be able to finish that.

552
00:23:49,257 --> 00:23:51,125
- No.
- Where are you from?

553
00:23:51,360 --> 00:23:52,459
I'm from Seoul.

554
00:23:52,460 --> 00:23:55,295
I haven't heard "divvy it up" in a while.

555
00:23:56,298 --> 00:23:57,832
Let's all share where we're from.

556
00:23:58,033 --> 00:24:00,333
- That's probably okay to share.
- We can at least share that.

557
00:24:00,334 --> 00:24:02,869
I'll go first, then.

558
00:24:02,870 --> 00:24:04,738
I'm from Gwangju, Jeollanam-do.

559
00:24:05,842 --> 00:24:08,843
Now I'm just working...

560
00:24:08,844 --> 00:24:10,111
here and there.

561
00:24:11,113 --> 00:24:13,279
Dark Knight looks really intimidating,

562
00:24:13,280 --> 00:24:15,985
like he could shoot someone
without blinking.

563
00:24:15,986 --> 00:24:17,650
He looks intense.

564
00:24:17,651 --> 00:24:19,686
Everything about him was dark.

565
00:24:19,687 --> 00:24:21,855
If I ever came across him
on a battlefield, I'd be dead.

566
00:24:21,856 --> 00:24:24,524
He looks like he could take out 30 people
without getting a scratch.

567
00:24:24,525 --> 00:24:26,693
I thought he wouldn't be easy.

568
00:24:26,694 --> 00:24:28,962
I was born in Seoul.

569
00:24:28,963 --> 00:24:30,263
I live here and there.

570
00:24:30,466 --> 00:24:32,466
I move around a lot.

571
00:24:32,768 --> 00:24:34,301
I don't know why you look so familiar.

572
00:24:34,302 --> 00:24:35,169
- Me?
- Yes.

573
00:24:35,170 --> 00:24:37,638
- You might not be wrong!
- Really?

574
00:24:37,639 --> 00:24:39,273
People say I look like someone a lot.

575
00:24:39,506 --> 00:24:41,007
- Shin Hae Sun!
- Who?

576
00:24:41,008 --> 00:24:41,908
Shin Hae Sun.

577
00:24:41,909 --> 00:24:44,511
I'm so flattered. I never got that before.

578
00:24:44,512 --> 00:24:46,413
I love that.

579
00:24:49,237 --> 00:24:51,218
Actually, I'm from New York.

580
00:24:51,854 --> 00:24:53,419
I was born in New York.

581
00:24:53,420 --> 00:24:56,690
I came to Korea eight years ago.

582
00:24:56,691 --> 00:24:57,725
I think I've seen him somewhere.

583
00:24:57,726 --> 00:24:59,894
I know. I'm sure I saw him somewhere.

584
00:24:59,895 --> 00:25:02,062
I might have a familiar face.

585
00:25:03,598 --> 00:25:04,498
Michael?

586
00:25:04,899 --> 00:25:08,735
He seemed to be really studying
each person.

587
00:25:08,970 --> 00:25:10,070
He kind of scared me.

588
00:25:10,071 --> 00:25:12,139
My first impression
of Michael wasn't great.

589
00:25:13,241 --> 00:25:14,241
He looks ruthless.

590
00:25:14,877 --> 00:25:16,310
As for Michael,

591
00:25:16,311 --> 00:25:19,013
it seemed like he was trying...

592
00:25:19,014 --> 00:25:20,814
to build this image...

593
00:25:20,815 --> 00:25:23,317
of being intelligent
and part of the upper class.

594
00:25:23,651 --> 00:25:26,086
But I felt like I could see something
underneath that.

595
00:25:26,355 --> 00:25:28,355
I don't think he's as harmless
as he seems.

596
00:25:28,356 --> 00:25:30,557
I'm not sure...

597
00:25:30,558 --> 00:25:33,527
if our views are similar,

598
00:25:33,929 --> 00:25:36,797
but I thought having him as an ally...

599
00:25:36,798 --> 00:25:37,864
could be helpful.

600
00:25:38,500 --> 00:25:40,001
What part of New York are you from?

601
00:25:40,002 --> 00:25:41,535
I'm from the Lower East Side.

602
00:25:41,536 --> 00:25:42,769
- The Lower East Side.
- The Lower East Side.

603
00:25:44,606 --> 00:25:47,207
I want to know where that is.

604
00:25:47,208 --> 00:25:49,309
I left my name tag in my room.

605
00:25:49,310 --> 00:25:50,777
I'm Benjamin.

606
00:25:51,112 --> 00:25:52,579
I was born in Seoul.

607
00:25:52,580 --> 00:25:53,980
I live in Seoul now too.

608
00:25:54,249 --> 00:25:56,149
Have you lived overseas?

609
00:25:56,150 --> 00:25:57,250
A little.

610
00:25:57,419 --> 00:25:59,720
- I'll tell you all about it later.
- Okay.

611
00:25:59,721 --> 00:26:00,754
All right.

612
00:26:01,490 --> 00:26:05,993
Benjamin speaks a little bit
of two languages.

613
00:26:05,994 --> 00:26:10,698
He was born in Korea, so unfortunately,

614
00:26:10,699 --> 00:26:11,866
he doesn't have US citizenship.

615
00:26:12,171 --> 00:26:15,769
He had to move back and forth...

616
00:26:15,770 --> 00:26:17,671
because of his parents' visa issues.

617
00:26:17,672 --> 00:26:20,474
He never really got a proper education,

618
00:26:20,475 --> 00:26:23,877
so his Korean is a little awkward,
and his English isn't fluent either.

619
00:26:23,878 --> 00:26:25,679
That's the backstory.

620
00:26:26,147 --> 00:26:29,683
If people think I have a language barrier,

621
00:26:29,684 --> 00:26:32,018
they might not take me seriously.

622
00:26:32,019 --> 00:26:34,188
That's why I created Benjamin.

623
00:26:34,189 --> 00:26:37,190
Even if I wasn't playing Benjamin,

624
00:26:37,191 --> 00:26:38,358
I probably wouldn't talk much.

625
00:26:39,060 --> 00:26:41,661
I don't think you need to talk a lot...

626
00:26:41,662 --> 00:26:44,865
in a setting like this.

627
00:26:44,866 --> 00:26:48,668
When Michael said he was born in New York,

628
00:26:49,438 --> 00:26:50,370
I thought I was screwed.

629
00:26:50,371 --> 00:26:51,972
That's not good for Benjamin.

630
00:26:51,973 --> 00:26:53,607
He's only been to the US for a month.

631
00:26:53,608 --> 00:26:57,711
I thought Benjamin would be...

632
00:26:57,712 --> 00:27:01,815
pretty open-minded about homosexuality.

633
00:27:02,417 --> 00:27:04,584
People who've lived in the US
for a long time...

634
00:27:05,186 --> 00:27:06,720
have a certain vibe.

635
00:27:06,721 --> 00:27:09,022
He has a slightly feminine vibe.

636
00:27:09,691 --> 00:27:12,326
I was born in Seoul.

637
00:27:12,660 --> 00:27:15,362
I only lived there until I was four,
so I don't remember much.

638
00:27:15,763 --> 00:27:18,798
After that,
I lived in Gyeonggi-do for a while.

639
00:27:19,334 --> 00:27:21,735
It was hard to pretend I didn't know her.

640
00:27:22,070 --> 00:27:23,771
Polar Bear and I have been...

641
00:27:24,139 --> 00:27:26,707
on a lot of shows together
and see each other pretty often.

642
00:27:26,708 --> 00:27:28,676
It was awkward to pretend.

643
00:27:29,310 --> 00:27:32,879
I didn't know what expression
I was supposed to have.

644
00:27:32,880 --> 00:27:34,948
There's no real anonymity between us.

645
00:27:36,718 --> 00:27:38,687
I was born in Seoul,
and I've lived here my whole life.

646
00:27:38,688 --> 00:27:40,521
I plan to stay in Seoul
for the rest of my life too.

647
00:27:41,256 --> 00:27:43,824
I've never lived anywhere else,
and I don't plan to.

648
00:27:46,454 --> 00:27:48,262
What Superman was doing...

649
00:27:48,263 --> 00:27:51,365
When men first meet,

650
00:27:51,366 --> 00:27:54,335
you can kind of get a vibe
from each other.

651
00:27:54,336 --> 00:27:55,635
He just seems tough.

652
00:27:55,636 --> 00:27:57,938
He seems kind of old-school.

653
00:27:59,040 --> 00:28:02,175
I was born in Seoul,
and I still live in Seoul.

654
00:28:04,379 --> 00:28:05,779
She's alluring.

655
00:28:06,147 --> 00:28:08,582
I could tell she had seductive energy.

656
00:28:08,583 --> 00:28:11,284
There's something about her
that captivates people.

657
00:28:11,285 --> 00:28:13,320
She was wearing heavy makeup.

658
00:28:13,321 --> 00:28:14,588
It must be a pain to take off.

659
00:28:15,557 --> 00:28:18,160
It's pretty concentrated.

660
00:28:18,161 --> 00:28:19,926
Most of us are from similar areas.

661
00:28:21,396 --> 00:28:22,396
Right.

662
00:28:22,697 --> 00:28:23,563
Then...

663
00:28:24,265 --> 00:28:26,767
Finally!

664
00:28:26,768 --> 00:28:27,701
Here we go.

665
00:28:28,069 --> 00:28:29,436
(Residents' pre-interview comments)

666
00:28:29,437 --> 00:28:33,073
Welcome to the Community House.

667
00:28:33,908 --> 00:28:36,476
To help you understand each other better,

668
00:28:36,877 --> 00:28:39,780
we'll be revealing
some of your honest thoughts.

669
00:28:39,781 --> 00:28:40,646
That's fun.

670
00:28:41,182 --> 00:28:43,617
Your identities will remain anonymous.

671
00:28:44,152 --> 00:28:45,185
I see.

672
00:28:48,623 --> 00:28:51,492
"Homosexuality is an acquired defect."

673
00:28:56,365 --> 00:29:00,167
"In Korea, men are the ones
being discriminated against."

674
00:29:03,638 --> 00:29:06,506
"The inheritance tax should be higher."

675
00:29:11,079 --> 00:29:13,447
"Fighting poverty is
the government's responsibility."

676
00:29:20,521 --> 00:29:24,991
"The minimum wage just makes
people more dependent."

677
00:29:31,166 --> 00:29:34,968
"People who don't compete deserve
to be left behind."

678
00:29:43,611 --> 00:29:45,078
I was shocked.

679
00:29:45,079 --> 00:29:49,783
Some people here have views
that are totally opposite to mine,

680
00:29:49,784 --> 00:29:53,253
and they feel so strongly about them.

681
00:29:54,188 --> 00:29:56,290
People like that are here with me.

682
00:29:56,691 --> 00:30:00,127
Right. We're all sitting here
laughing and joking around,

683
00:30:00,128 --> 00:30:02,863
but we actually have very different views.

684
00:30:03,331 --> 00:30:06,099
"People like that are actually here.
They're among us."

685
00:30:07,002 --> 00:30:08,201
Who could they be?

686
00:30:08,903 --> 00:30:10,270
The first comment was,

687
00:30:10,805 --> 00:30:12,806
"Homosexuality is an acquired defect."

688
00:30:12,807 --> 00:30:14,475
It was too harsh.

689
00:30:15,243 --> 00:30:17,311
I thought some of those comments
could be hurtful.

690
00:30:17,513 --> 00:30:20,714
In my experience, men tend to...

691
00:30:20,715 --> 00:30:23,717
have a stronger reaction...

692
00:30:23,718 --> 00:30:27,321
against homosexuality than women do.

693
00:30:27,664 --> 00:30:30,758
That's probably from Gray or Superman.

694
00:30:31,492 --> 00:30:35,195
It sounded like something you'd hear
from a very masculine guy.

695
00:30:35,530 --> 00:30:38,498
It could be Dark Knight...

696
00:30:38,634 --> 00:30:39,700
or Superman.

697
00:30:40,102 --> 00:30:43,637
"The inheritance tax should be higher"
made my jaw drop.

698
00:30:44,472 --> 00:30:45,772
How much higher?

699
00:30:45,773 --> 00:30:48,474
It's like, "Give it all back
and get out of here."

700
00:30:48,475 --> 00:30:51,812
Who even came up with that idea?

701
00:30:52,180 --> 00:30:54,181
"People who don't compete deserve
to be left behind."

702
00:30:54,182 --> 00:30:57,151
That's how the history of humanity
and all living things has worked.

703
00:30:57,485 --> 00:31:00,153
That might sound pretty harsh or cold.

704
00:31:00,688 --> 00:31:04,758
If you look at reality objectively,

705
00:31:05,059 --> 00:31:07,060
we develop through constant competition.

706
00:31:07,362 --> 00:31:09,296
When someone falls behind,

707
00:31:09,297 --> 00:31:10,831
they push themselves even harder...

708
00:31:11,032 --> 00:31:13,700
to catch up and end up surpassing
the people ahead of them.

709
00:31:13,701 --> 00:31:16,269
I don't think
the statement itself is wrong.

710
00:31:16,639 --> 00:31:20,974
"The minimum wage just makes
people more dependent."

711
00:31:20,975 --> 00:31:24,745
Someone among us thinks that way.

712
00:31:24,746 --> 00:31:26,613
I don't think it's just one person.

713
00:31:27,091 --> 00:31:30,825
When I imagine the kind of world
I want to live in,

714
00:31:31,318 --> 00:31:34,354
I want to be in a place...

715
00:31:34,855 --> 00:31:36,623
where I'm not miserable or afraid,

716
00:31:36,624 --> 00:31:40,594
no matter what kind of person I am.

717
00:31:40,895 --> 00:31:43,798
I want to live in a world...

718
00:31:43,799 --> 00:31:45,432
that doesn't feel so scary...

719
00:31:45,879 --> 00:31:47,781
even if I imagine myself...

720
00:31:48,269 --> 00:31:50,904
as gay, disabled, or in a wheelchair.

721
00:31:51,339 --> 00:31:53,774
Encouraging that kind of competition...

722
00:31:53,775 --> 00:31:57,910
and leaving people without a safety net...

723
00:31:57,911 --> 00:31:59,880
makes people miserable.

724
00:32:01,550 --> 00:32:02,649
All of us.

725
00:32:09,657 --> 00:32:10,857
(Today's leader selection)

726
00:32:11,259 --> 00:32:12,659
Today's leader?

727
00:32:13,628 --> 00:32:17,364
We will now select today's leader.

728
00:32:17,932 --> 00:32:20,934
Discuss among yourselves freely...

729
00:32:21,302 --> 00:32:23,603
and choose today's leader.

730
00:32:24,272 --> 00:32:27,107
The Community Center
in the central plaza...

731
00:32:27,108 --> 00:32:29,843
will open in one hour.

732
00:32:29,844 --> 00:32:31,612
Once the leader has been selected,

733
00:32:31,979 --> 00:32:34,747
the leader must go
to the Community Center...

734
00:32:34,748 --> 00:32:36,883
and register as today's leader.

735
00:32:37,985 --> 00:32:40,520
(Today's leader selection)

736
00:32:42,523 --> 00:32:45,325
It says "today's leader,"
so I guess the leader changes every day.

737
00:32:45,326 --> 00:32:46,360
- I think so.
- Every day.

738
00:32:46,361 --> 00:32:47,994
It sounds like it will change every day.

739
00:32:47,995 --> 00:32:49,229
(A new leader is chosen once a day.)

740
00:32:50,370 --> 00:32:51,230
Are there 12 of us?

741
00:32:51,231 --> 00:32:52,699
Six each.

742
00:32:52,700 --> 00:32:55,001
Six men and six women.

743
00:32:55,002 --> 00:32:56,636
So we're 12?

744
00:32:56,871 --> 00:32:58,238
We have nine days in total.

745
00:32:58,239 --> 00:32:59,206
Yes, nine days.

746
00:32:59,207 --> 00:33:01,442
What does the leader have to do?

747
00:33:01,443 --> 00:33:03,444
I looked at the map earlier,

748
00:33:03,445 --> 00:33:07,347
and there was a supermarket
and a notice board.

749
00:33:07,782 --> 00:33:09,282
There's a menu over there.

750
00:33:09,650 --> 00:33:12,085
- Shall we bring it here?
- Let's do that.

751
00:33:12,520 --> 00:33:14,821
When it comes to spending money...

752
00:33:14,822 --> 00:33:18,893
I think the leader gets to decide
what we spend the money on.

753
00:33:20,294 --> 00:33:22,395
- Thank you.
- A slice of cake is 200 dollars.

754
00:33:22,396 --> 00:33:23,529
It's 200 dollars?

755
00:33:23,530 --> 00:33:24,998
This is no joke.

756
00:33:24,999 --> 00:33:27,434
- What?
- Wow.

757
00:33:27,435 --> 00:33:30,136
The currency in this community is...

758
00:33:30,471 --> 00:33:31,838
basically like Venezuela.

759
00:33:32,373 --> 00:33:33,239
It's ridiculous.

760
00:33:33,741 --> 00:33:35,642
It's so expensive. Give us a discount.

761
00:33:36,377 --> 00:33:38,478
"Only the leader can buy things."

762
00:33:38,479 --> 00:33:39,713
(Only the leader can purchase things.)

763
00:33:39,714 --> 00:33:42,882
Since everything is quite expensive,

764
00:33:42,883 --> 00:33:44,317
we'll have different opinions.

765
00:33:45,052 --> 00:33:48,021
Some people might just want
to get 12 packs of ramyeon.

766
00:33:48,422 --> 00:33:51,557
Some might want 3kg of pork belly
and soju.

767
00:33:51,558 --> 00:33:52,559
This is interesting.

768
00:33:52,560 --> 00:33:55,195
I think alcohol and cigarettes could be...

769
00:33:55,196 --> 00:33:58,030
a big source of conflict.

770
00:33:58,031 --> 00:33:59,466
Does anyone here smoke?

771
00:33:59,467 --> 00:34:00,933
- You're right.
- Does anyone here smoke?

772
00:34:00,934 --> 00:34:02,034
Are there any smokers?

773
00:34:02,035 --> 00:34:03,069
I vape.

774
00:34:03,070 --> 00:34:04,137
(Smokers: Gray, Michael)

775
00:34:04,138 --> 00:34:06,105
- I smoke too.
- Is that so?

776
00:34:06,274 --> 00:34:08,342
Three? Relatively, that's not that bad.

777
00:34:08,343 --> 00:34:10,143
But it's quite expensive.

778
00:34:10,144 --> 00:34:11,045
(A pack of cigarettes: 500 dollars)

779
00:34:11,046 --> 00:34:12,446
It's expensive.

780
00:34:14,216 --> 00:34:15,549
It's 500 dollars.

781
00:34:15,550 --> 00:34:18,151
Anyway, today's leader gets...

782
00:34:18,152 --> 00:34:20,354
to decide what we order.

783
00:34:20,355 --> 00:34:21,221
- Right.
- Yes.

784
00:34:21,222 --> 00:34:23,890
Let's assume we all want the job...

785
00:34:23,891 --> 00:34:25,826
and we're all candidates.

786
00:34:25,827 --> 00:34:28,095
Why don't we go around
and each say one thing we'd do...

787
00:34:28,096 --> 00:34:29,129
if we got elected?

788
00:34:30,498 --> 00:34:31,932
I don't want to be the leader today.

789
00:34:32,133 --> 00:34:34,134
Since it's the first day,
it might be too much pressure.

790
00:34:34,135 --> 00:34:35,769
- I understand.
- I'm too nervous.

791
00:34:35,770 --> 00:34:37,237
Me too. I'd probably mess it up.

792
00:34:38,039 --> 00:34:40,206
Whoever can do the best job
should be the leader.

793
00:34:41,208 --> 00:34:42,975
When I went up to my room,

794
00:34:42,976 --> 00:34:44,745
I was shocked by my test result.

795
00:34:44,746 --> 00:34:46,179
I got 11 points?

796
00:34:46,614 --> 00:34:47,847
That was shocking.

797
00:34:48,550 --> 00:34:50,184
I must hide my score.

798
00:34:50,185 --> 00:34:52,719
That was my top priority.

799
00:34:52,720 --> 00:34:53,754
My heart started pounding.

800
00:34:54,655 --> 00:34:55,788
It was racing.

801
00:34:56,490 --> 00:34:58,658
I was dumb enough to say
I didn't want to be the leader.

802
00:34:59,661 --> 00:35:00,994
I regretted it later.

803
00:35:00,995 --> 00:35:03,397
I didn't have to be so defensive.

804
00:35:03,698 --> 00:35:06,466
I'm not living up to my name, Hippo.

805
00:35:06,467 --> 00:35:08,101
Benjamin shouldn't try to take charge.

806
00:35:08,336 --> 00:35:10,737
If his Korean isn't that good,
he can't be a leader.

807
00:35:10,738 --> 00:35:12,839
I was glad. I wanted to run.

808
00:35:13,041 --> 00:35:14,808
I was glad they weren't going to run.

809
00:35:15,476 --> 00:35:17,677
You two don't want to run today.

810
00:35:17,678 --> 00:35:20,513
"It's just the first day,
but it sounds like fun."

811
00:35:20,514 --> 00:35:21,414
Does anyone else think so?

812
00:35:23,284 --> 00:35:25,085
To be honest, it might be fun.

813
00:35:25,086 --> 00:35:26,219
Michael.

814
00:35:26,220 --> 00:35:27,287
Michael is in.

815
00:35:27,788 --> 00:35:29,756
I'm all about trying things out.

816
00:35:29,757 --> 00:35:30,656
Nangja.

817
00:35:30,657 --> 00:35:31,724
(Nangja is running.)

818
00:35:31,725 --> 00:35:33,192
I recommend Ted.

819
00:35:33,662 --> 00:35:34,961
- Me?
- I recommend you.

820
00:35:36,497 --> 00:35:37,564
Okay.

821
00:35:37,865 --> 00:35:39,299
Thanks for recommending me.

822
00:35:39,634 --> 00:35:41,168
I'm a little torn.

823
00:35:41,636 --> 00:35:42,703
If you're hesitating, just go for it.

824
00:35:43,236 --> 00:35:46,072
Right. I have to be chosen anyway.

825
00:35:46,641 --> 00:35:47,507
I'm in.

826
00:35:47,508 --> 00:35:48,741
Genie is running too.

827
00:35:48,742 --> 00:35:49,676
All right.

828
00:35:49,744 --> 00:35:52,512
(Four Candidates for Day One Leader)

829
00:35:52,513 --> 00:35:56,149
(Michael, Nangja, Ted, Genie)

830
00:35:56,550 --> 00:35:59,052
Actually, I'm not sure...

831
00:35:59,654 --> 00:36:01,721
I'd do a good job if I became the leader.

832
00:36:03,191 --> 00:36:04,490
But I'll feel things out.

833
00:36:06,494 --> 00:36:07,961
I've led plenty of times before...

834
00:36:08,163 --> 00:36:11,630
at multilateral meetings
and international events.

835
00:36:11,631 --> 00:36:13,800
I've dealt
with a lot of conflicting interests...

836
00:36:13,801 --> 00:36:15,569
and been through lots of conflicts.

837
00:36:15,703 --> 00:36:18,338
I'm confident I could lead people too.

838
00:36:18,606 --> 00:36:21,175
But I'll probably come across
as the weakest person here.

839
00:36:21,176 --> 00:36:22,809
And that's exactly the image I want.

840
00:36:22,810 --> 00:36:24,544
I want to seem like an easygoing person.

841
00:36:24,545 --> 00:36:26,848
Someone they'd want on their side.

842
00:36:26,849 --> 00:36:28,783
Maybe even someone...

843
00:36:28,784 --> 00:36:30,317
who seems a little scatterbrained.

844
00:36:30,752 --> 00:36:32,352
I feel the same way.

845
00:36:32,353 --> 00:36:36,556
I feel like the leader is probably going
to be evaluated in some kind of order.

846
00:36:36,557 --> 00:36:38,626
Since it's the first day, it's probably
okay to make some mistakes...

847
00:36:38,627 --> 00:36:40,460
- since we all know nothing.
- Right.

848
00:36:40,461 --> 00:36:41,661
So, in that sense,

849
00:36:41,662 --> 00:36:44,164
it might be safer to be the leader
on the first day.

850
00:36:44,165 --> 00:36:46,699
I'm going to treat it like a demo run.

851
00:36:48,369 --> 00:36:50,136
- You seem so amiable.
- Sorry?

852
00:36:50,637 --> 00:36:51,542
- Do I?
- Yes.

853
00:36:51,543 --> 00:36:53,639
He looks like a natural group leader.

854
00:36:53,640 --> 00:36:54,774
Even his voice...

855
00:36:54,775 --> 00:36:56,075
sounds like a leader.

856
00:36:57,445 --> 00:37:00,747
I was more wary of her than I expected.

857
00:37:01,182 --> 00:37:02,114
I'm not sure.

858
00:37:02,115 --> 00:37:03,149
Superman.

859
00:37:03,617 --> 00:37:05,819
He gives me a weird look
whenever he looks at me.

860
00:37:06,654 --> 00:37:09,089
I feel like he doesn't like
people like me.

861
00:37:09,423 --> 00:37:10,356
I can feel it.

862
00:37:10,825 --> 00:37:14,795
At first, you try to size people up.

863
00:37:14,796 --> 00:37:17,964
When everyone has to hide
their true identity like this,

864
00:37:18,365 --> 00:37:20,267
people will deliberately try to put on...

865
00:37:20,434 --> 00:37:22,769
the image they want people to see.

866
00:37:23,037 --> 00:37:24,371
But she seemed so pure...

867
00:37:24,372 --> 00:37:26,039
that it caught me off guard.

868
00:37:26,173 --> 00:37:28,608
That made it harder for me
to figure her out.

869
00:37:28,609 --> 00:37:30,276
It put me on guard around her.

870
00:37:31,412 --> 00:37:35,081
Let me tell you about my experience.

871
00:37:35,082 --> 00:37:38,251
I've been on a lot of competition shows
like this.

872
00:37:38,419 --> 00:37:41,121
So I'm pretty experimental
and challenging...

873
00:37:41,122 --> 00:37:42,655
and like taking on challenges,

874
00:37:42,656 --> 00:37:43,823
which comes with responsibility.

875
00:37:43,824 --> 00:37:46,526
I take full responsibility
for my decisions.

876
00:37:46,976 --> 00:37:48,895
I have a lot of experience
with games like this,

877
00:37:49,129 --> 00:37:50,596
and I'm an expert.

878
00:37:50,866 --> 00:37:53,066
I figured being the leader...

879
00:37:53,067 --> 00:37:56,638
would have more benefits
than disadvantages.

880
00:37:56,639 --> 00:37:58,271
I'd be able to get information first.

881
00:37:58,606 --> 00:38:02,808
In a game like this, information is key.

882
00:38:03,778 --> 00:38:04,711
I see.

883
00:38:06,547 --> 00:38:07,881
The speeches got better and better.

884
00:38:08,015 --> 00:38:10,617
The speeches were getting
better and better,

885
00:38:10,785 --> 00:38:12,185
so I'm feeling the pressure.

886
00:38:12,486 --> 00:38:15,721
I think the biggest issue...

887
00:38:15,722 --> 00:38:18,124
we have right now is food.

888
00:38:18,392 --> 00:38:19,759
And I take food very seriously.

889
00:38:20,394 --> 00:38:23,162
So I'll do my best to make sure...

890
00:38:23,163 --> 00:38:25,098
everyone gets to enjoy their meals.

891
00:38:25,099 --> 00:38:26,232
That was the best.

892
00:38:27,869 --> 00:38:29,402
Vote for her!

893
00:38:29,403 --> 00:38:31,537
- Do you two want to go again?
- I know!

894
00:38:31,538 --> 00:38:34,006
- I'm not a good cook, so I don't know...
- I'm going to withdraw!

895
00:38:34,007 --> 00:38:35,508
The only thing I can make is ramyeon.

896
00:38:36,243 --> 00:38:38,011
All right.

897
00:38:38,012 --> 00:38:39,946
- Please pass this around.
- Okay.

898
00:38:41,448 --> 00:38:43,582
She made a really good promise
at just the right time.

899
00:38:43,583 --> 00:38:45,151
"A leader shouldn't let people starve."

900
00:38:45,519 --> 00:38:47,753
Everyone was hungry,

901
00:38:47,856 --> 00:38:50,056
and she came up with a short but really
effective pitch at the right moment.

902
00:38:50,624 --> 00:38:53,559
It's been a long time
since I've voted on something like this.

903
00:38:53,560 --> 00:38:55,861
- Is everyone done?
- I won't let it show.

904
00:38:55,862 --> 00:39:00,633
(Voting for Day One Leader)

905
00:39:00,634 --> 00:39:01,834
Let's see the first vote.

906
00:39:02,870 --> 00:39:03,870
Genie.

907
00:39:04,338 --> 00:39:05,305
Genie.

908
00:39:05,306 --> 00:39:06,572
(Genie: one)

909
00:39:06,573 --> 00:39:07,840
It says "four."

910
00:39:07,841 --> 00:39:08,708
- Genie.
- Genie.

911
00:39:08,709 --> 00:39:09,909
They also put a number.

912
00:39:09,910 --> 00:39:11,744
(Genie: two)

913
00:39:11,769 --> 00:39:12,803
Genie.

914
00:39:14,248 --> 00:39:15,148
Michael.

915
00:39:15,149 --> 00:39:16,583
(Michael: 1, Genie: 3)

916
00:39:16,608 --> 00:39:17,708
Genie.

917
00:39:18,519 --> 00:39:19,819
Michael.

918
00:39:20,087 --> 00:39:21,054
Genie.

919
00:39:22,256 --> 00:39:23,189
Genie.

920
00:39:23,190 --> 00:39:24,824
(Michael: 2, Genie: 6)

921
00:39:26,160 --> 00:39:28,461
Suddenly, I want at least one vote.

922
00:39:28,996 --> 00:39:29,963
Am I being greedy?

923
00:39:30,232 --> 00:39:31,231
Ted.

924
00:39:32,199 --> 00:39:33,766
I don't know who that is, but thanks.

925
00:39:33,767 --> 00:39:35,735
If you only get one vote, that's not good.

926
00:39:36,303 --> 00:39:37,470
That's your own vote.

927
00:39:37,471 --> 00:39:39,105
- Michael.
- I don't mind that.

928
00:39:39,106 --> 00:39:40,606
(Ted: 1, Michael: 3, Genie: 6)

929
00:39:40,607 --> 00:39:41,874
I've healed emotionally.

930
00:39:41,875 --> 00:39:42,875
Right.

931
00:39:42,876 --> 00:39:43,776
Genie.

932
00:39:43,777 --> 00:39:44,910
(Ted: 1, Michael: 3, Genie: 7)

933
00:39:44,911 --> 00:39:46,579
Campaign promises do matter.

934
00:39:46,580 --> 00:39:48,214
You don't vote for someone for no reason.

935
00:39:48,215 --> 00:39:49,215
Genie.

936
00:39:49,216 --> 00:39:51,016
(Ted: 1, Michael: 3, Genie: 8)

937
00:39:51,041 --> 00:39:52,175
- So...
- Thank you.

938
00:39:52,420 --> 00:39:53,820
You should've voted for yourself.

939
00:39:53,821 --> 00:39:54,854
But I didn't.

940
00:39:55,322 --> 00:39:58,625
Genie became today's leader
with eight votes.

941
00:39:58,626 --> 00:40:00,627
(Day one leader: Genie)

942
00:40:01,061 --> 00:40:03,562
(If you got zero votes,
you didn't even vote for yourself.)

943
00:40:03,798 --> 00:40:05,432
I felt like people wouldn't vote for me.

944
00:40:05,433 --> 00:40:06,800
I would've hated getting just one vote.

945
00:40:06,801 --> 00:40:08,201
Someone had one vote, right?

946
00:40:08,202 --> 00:40:09,402
I got one vote.

947
00:40:09,403 --> 00:40:11,037
I wonder who that was.

948
00:40:11,372 --> 00:40:14,974
Gray recommended me, so it could be him.

949
00:40:15,210 --> 00:40:17,676
I voted for Michael.

950
00:40:17,677 --> 00:40:21,614
I liked that he'd been
on a lot of survival shows.

951
00:40:21,615 --> 00:40:23,449
Please tell Ted I'm sorry.

952
00:40:23,952 --> 00:40:25,351
I voted for Ted.

953
00:40:25,352 --> 00:40:28,021
I felt like he played a big role...

954
00:40:28,022 --> 00:40:29,489
in helping everyone get along.

955
00:40:29,490 --> 00:40:30,623
I chose Michael...

956
00:40:30,624 --> 00:40:32,459
to tell him I voted for him.

957
00:40:32,493 --> 00:40:33,493
To earn his trust.

958
00:40:33,494 --> 00:40:36,196
I was a little disappointed,
but at the same time,

959
00:40:36,563 --> 00:40:39,965
I wondered if my whole
"playing dumb" strategy actually worked.

960
00:40:39,966 --> 00:40:42,900
And it was unexpected
that votes piled up like this...

961
00:40:42,901 --> 00:40:44,937
just because of how they come across.

962
00:40:47,641 --> 00:40:48,975
I was so happy.

963
00:40:48,976 --> 00:40:51,010
(Genie: right, feminism,
upper-middle, conservative)

964
00:40:51,011 --> 00:40:54,513
I knew everyone would take food seriously.

965
00:40:55,508 --> 00:40:58,643
I guess I was wittier than I thought.

966
00:41:00,187 --> 00:41:04,056
(The Community Center)

967
00:41:05,426 --> 00:41:11,664
(Genie, day one leader)

968
00:41:11,699 --> 00:41:14,100
Congratulations on becoming the leader.

969
00:41:14,468 --> 00:41:17,003
- This is fascinating!
- Please collect your leader's box.

970
00:41:19,139 --> 00:41:20,573
I had a feeling...

971
00:41:20,574 --> 00:41:23,609
the leader would get benefits...

972
00:41:23,610 --> 00:41:25,010
or some kind of extra prize later...

973
00:41:25,011 --> 00:41:27,114
to make everyone want to be a leader.

974
00:41:27,115 --> 00:41:28,747
Leader's advantages!

975
00:41:28,748 --> 00:41:31,350
There was a leader's box.

976
00:41:31,375 --> 00:41:34,144
There was a notice for the leader
in the box.

977
00:41:34,888 --> 00:41:37,523
A pretty gold leader badge too.

978
00:41:37,524 --> 00:41:40,492
There was something
that made me really glad...

979
00:41:40,493 --> 00:41:43,163
that I decided to run today.

980
00:41:43,164 --> 00:41:45,598
A golden immunity card.

981
00:41:46,266 --> 00:41:49,269
The leader gets one immunity card,

982
00:41:49,270 --> 00:41:53,272
and they can also transfer it
to someone else.

983
00:41:53,674 --> 00:41:55,808
Usually, when you get a benefit,

984
00:41:55,809 --> 00:41:58,945
there's some kind of disadvantage.

985
00:41:58,946 --> 00:42:00,113
Of course.

986
00:42:00,348 --> 00:42:04,150
You can only transfer
the immunity card once,

987
00:42:04,752 --> 00:42:07,454
and even if you become leader again,

988
00:42:07,455 --> 00:42:09,222
you don't get another one.

989
00:42:09,790 --> 00:42:11,758
It's not going to be simple.

990
00:42:11,894 --> 00:42:13,460
There's always a catch.

991
00:42:13,994 --> 00:42:15,862
We don't have time,
so the immunity card...

992
00:42:15,863 --> 00:42:17,263
If you want to transfer the card...

993
00:42:17,264 --> 00:42:19,265
Those who have immunity cards...

994
00:42:19,266 --> 00:42:21,337
Why would I use my immunity card?

995
00:42:21,338 --> 00:42:23,403
Genie, cut out the act.

996
00:42:23,404 --> 00:42:24,770
What do I do?

997
00:42:24,806 --> 00:42:27,373
You shouldn't force someone to do this.

998
00:42:27,374 --> 00:42:29,576
(Immunity Card)

999
00:42:30,243 --> 00:42:31,945
She's coming back.

1000
00:42:31,946 --> 00:42:34,114
- I'm nervous.
- There's some kind of kit.

1001
00:42:34,115 --> 00:42:35,147
My heart is racing.

1002
00:42:36,050 --> 00:42:37,050
The leader is coming in.

1003
00:42:40,821 --> 00:42:42,588
They give you a leader's box like this.

1004
00:42:44,792 --> 00:42:46,159
In this box...

1005
00:42:47,227 --> 00:42:49,595
A special badge to wear
throughout my term.

1006
00:42:49,763 --> 00:42:51,097
- It's gold!
- It is.

1007
00:42:51,098 --> 00:42:52,231
(A golden name tag for the leader)

1008
00:42:52,400 --> 00:42:54,500
I didn't show them the immunity card.

1009
00:42:54,501 --> 00:42:56,903
I just shared the leader's rules.

1010
00:42:56,904 --> 00:42:59,539
I shared the fact that the leader gets...

1011
00:42:59,540 --> 00:43:01,074
an immunity card.

1012
00:43:01,075 --> 00:43:04,611
And they said that was...

1013
00:43:04,612 --> 00:43:06,246
a big advantage.

1014
00:43:06,513 --> 00:43:07,980
Being the leader is pretty good.

1015
00:43:08,148 --> 00:43:09,215
It has a lot of benefits.

1016
00:43:09,383 --> 00:43:13,453
Actually,
I didn't want to share that at first.

1017
00:43:13,587 --> 00:43:17,489
I thought I might become a target
if I did,

1018
00:43:17,824 --> 00:43:20,093
so I was a little reluctant.

1019
00:43:20,094 --> 00:43:22,562
But I figured that if someone else became
the leader the next day,

1020
00:43:22,563 --> 00:43:24,697
everyone would find out about it anyway.

1021
00:43:24,865 --> 00:43:27,099
I thought it was just a matter of time,

1022
00:43:27,534 --> 00:43:28,601
so I just shared it.

1023
00:43:30,405 --> 00:43:34,908
Next, here are the basic rules
of the Community House.

1024
00:43:36,477 --> 00:43:37,377
One.

1025
00:43:37,723 --> 00:43:40,813
A new leader is chosen every day,

1026
00:43:41,281 --> 00:43:43,183
with no limit on consecutive terms.

1027
00:43:44,451 --> 00:43:45,518
Two.

1028
00:43:45,519 --> 00:43:49,622
The leader has the authority
to make decisions...

1029
00:43:49,623 --> 00:43:50,990
about the community's
economic activities...

1030
00:43:51,525 --> 00:43:54,260
and may choose whether or not
to consult the residents.

1031
00:43:55,529 --> 00:43:57,363
- You can be a dictator too.
- Three.

1032
00:43:57,739 --> 00:44:01,908
There are daytime and nighttime activities
in the Community House.

1033
00:44:02,504 --> 00:44:04,704
You can build up your personal funds...

1034
00:44:04,729 --> 00:44:06,763
through various activities
during the day...

1035
00:44:07,133 --> 00:44:09,401
and anonymous chats at night.

1036
00:44:10,645 --> 00:44:12,913
The personal funds you accumulate...

1037
00:44:12,914 --> 00:44:15,114
will become your final prize money.

1038
00:44:15,348 --> 00:44:16,916
If you're eliminated before the end,

1039
00:44:16,917 --> 00:44:19,318
you lose all the money you've accumulated.

1040
00:44:19,319 --> 00:44:20,353
(If you don't get eliminated
along the way,)

1041
00:44:20,354 --> 00:44:21,253
(you receive your personal funds
as your prize money.)

1042
00:44:21,254 --> 00:44:22,254
Four.

1043
00:44:22,490 --> 00:44:24,924
Personal funds can only be saved up.

1044
00:44:25,425 --> 00:44:28,927
You can't transfer them to other residents
or use them to make purchases.

1045
00:44:29,831 --> 00:44:34,501
The Community House sincerely hopes...

1046
00:44:34,801 --> 00:44:38,670
all of you walk away
with a generous prize.

1047
00:44:40,641 --> 00:44:43,309
So, your prize money is
only the money you've saved up.

1048
00:44:43,410 --> 00:44:45,444
Correct.
As long as you don't get eliminated.

1049
00:44:47,858 --> 00:44:51,059
Now, we'll begin
distributing 20,000 dollars...

1050
00:44:51,084 --> 00:44:53,485
in personal funds.

1051
00:44:55,155 --> 00:44:57,990
Each resident will visit
the Community Center...

1052
00:44:57,991 --> 00:44:59,959
according to the order chosen
by the leader...

1053
00:45:00,327 --> 00:45:03,162
and claim whatever amount they want...

1054
00:45:03,163 --> 00:45:05,498
from the 20,000 dollars.

1055
00:45:05,499 --> 00:45:06,967
(Personal funds distribution)

1056
00:45:06,968 --> 00:45:08,635
Whatever amount they claim...

1057
00:45:08,636 --> 00:45:12,739
will become their initial personal funds
as long as there's still money left.

1058
00:45:13,406 --> 00:45:16,809
The leader has to go
to the Community Center...

1059
00:45:17,110 --> 00:45:19,778
and decide the order of distribution.

1060
00:45:20,581 --> 00:45:23,349
We're dividing 20,000 dollars
among ourselves.

1061
00:45:23,350 --> 00:45:25,351
It's like a loyalty test.

1062
00:45:25,352 --> 00:45:28,158
If someone claims the whole amount...

1063
00:45:28,159 --> 00:45:30,456
- They'll become everyone's enemy.
- Right.

1064
00:45:30,457 --> 00:45:33,259
Someone could make
a really selfish choice.

1065
00:45:33,928 --> 00:45:36,762
It could be chaotic from Day One.

1066
00:45:36,763 --> 00:45:38,364
It could be really messy.

1067
00:45:38,865 --> 00:45:40,633
I was freaking out.

1068
00:45:40,634 --> 00:45:43,635
It was a big decision to make.

1069
00:45:43,636 --> 00:45:46,038
I wanted to make sure...

1070
00:45:46,039 --> 00:45:48,807
everyone didn't end up resenting me.

1071
00:45:48,808 --> 00:45:52,344
But I had no idea
what order would be best for that.

1072
00:45:52,345 --> 00:45:53,545
That really stressed me out.

1073
00:45:55,483 --> 00:45:56,783
Since it's the first day,

1074
00:45:56,784 --> 00:45:59,519
why don't we just split it evenly 12 ways?

1075
00:45:59,654 --> 00:46:00,586
That would be...

1076
00:46:00,854 --> 00:46:03,556
around 1,660 to 1,670 dollars.

1077
00:46:03,757 --> 00:46:05,524
If we each take 1,660 to 1,670 dollars,

1078
00:46:05,525 --> 00:46:07,660
we can all get an equal share.

1079
00:46:10,998 --> 00:46:12,198
I'm just going to be clear.

1080
00:46:12,199 --> 00:46:13,299
I'll take 1,600 dollars.

1081
00:46:13,501 --> 00:46:15,034
- I'll put that amount.
- All of a sudden?

1082
00:46:15,802 --> 00:46:18,137
Put me first. I'll put 1,600 dollars.

1083
00:46:21,041 --> 00:46:22,141
I just wanted to go first.

1084
00:46:22,142 --> 00:46:24,243
I wanted to see
how everyone else would react.

1085
00:46:25,045 --> 00:46:26,745
He was creating...

1086
00:46:26,746 --> 00:46:29,782
his own game plan
and setting the tone for everyone.

1087
00:46:29,783 --> 00:46:32,818
He took the lead in shaping the situation.

1088
00:46:33,253 --> 00:46:34,320
First? Okay.

1089
00:46:34,321 --> 00:46:35,421
I'll go second.

1090
00:46:35,422 --> 00:46:36,522
I'll go third.

1091
00:46:36,857 --> 00:46:37,824
Number four.

1092
00:46:38,292 --> 00:46:39,692
Who wants to go fifth?

1093
00:46:40,093 --> 00:46:41,260
I'll go last.

1094
00:46:44,431 --> 00:46:46,565
There's always a risk...

1095
00:46:46,566 --> 00:46:48,534
in going last.

1096
00:46:48,535 --> 00:46:50,236
I thought that was cool.

1097
00:46:50,703 --> 00:46:53,707
Getting greedy right from the start...

1098
00:46:53,708 --> 00:46:55,040
would only make you a target.

1099
00:46:55,041 --> 00:46:58,344
He was smart enough to think about that.

1100
00:46:58,578 --> 00:47:01,246
I was confident that I wouldn't
be eliminated on the first day...

1101
00:47:01,247 --> 00:47:02,781
even if I didn't get any money.

1102
00:47:02,782 --> 00:47:04,483
That's why I deliberately chose
to go last.

1103
00:47:06,053 --> 00:47:08,254
I'll go second to last.

1104
00:47:08,555 --> 00:47:09,622
What's your nickname?

1105
00:47:10,123 --> 00:47:11,490
Polar Bear.

1106
00:47:16,296 --> 00:47:18,330
I let everyone pick the spot they wanted.

1107
00:47:18,732 --> 00:47:20,500
Once everyone had chosen,

1108
00:47:20,501 --> 00:47:22,468
I just took the spot that was left.

1109
00:47:22,469 --> 00:47:24,670
(Number nine: Genie)

1110
00:47:24,771 --> 00:47:26,105
Deep down,

1111
00:47:26,440 --> 00:47:27,906
I was really greedy.

1112
00:47:27,907 --> 00:47:29,776
I just wanted to go first...

1113
00:47:29,777 --> 00:47:31,577
and take the entire 20,000 dollars
for myself.

1114
00:47:32,145 --> 00:47:33,545
But I know...

1115
00:47:33,546 --> 00:47:35,414
if I make it look like...

1116
00:47:35,415 --> 00:47:39,251
I'm sacrificing for everyone now,
I'll get something back later.

1117
00:47:41,288 --> 00:47:45,758
(First person to claim their funds:
Dark Knight)

1118
00:47:46,027 --> 00:47:47,359
The money...

1119
00:47:48,028 --> 00:47:49,562
won't run out on the first day.

1120
00:47:49,798 --> 00:47:52,464
What if it says "There are no funds left"
when he comes back?

1121
00:47:53,202 --> 00:47:55,535
"The personal funds
have all been distributed."

1122
00:48:00,941 --> 00:48:02,141
(Please write down the amount you want.)

1123
00:48:02,142 --> 00:48:03,142
(Remaining amount: 20,000 dollars)

1124
00:48:12,486 --> 00:48:13,553
It's 1,600 dollars.

1125
00:48:15,155 --> 00:48:17,056
Please read the instructions.

1126
00:48:28,039 --> 00:48:29,068
They made it interesting.

1127
00:48:29,470 --> 00:48:30,536
- Why?
- Why?

1128
00:48:31,371 --> 00:48:32,905
They made it so we couldn't split
the money evenly.

1129
00:48:33,173 --> 00:48:35,440
It has to be in 1,000-dollar increments.

1130
00:48:38,412 --> 00:48:41,714
Then someone is bound to get less.

1131
00:48:42,849 --> 00:48:44,583
Please read the instructions.

1132
00:48:45,218 --> 00:48:49,755
(The funds can be claimed
in 1,000-dollar increments.)

1133
00:48:49,756 --> 00:48:50,722
In 1,000-dollar increments.

1134
00:48:52,593 --> 00:48:55,194
Even if you try to get less
and put 1,000 dollars,

1135
00:48:55,195 --> 00:48:57,363
you don't know what others would do.

1136
00:48:57,364 --> 00:48:59,599
Even if you put 1,000 dollars,
people might not believe you.

1137
00:49:03,570 --> 00:49:04,803
Earlier,

1138
00:49:04,804 --> 00:49:07,753
I looked through the stuff
about the community on my laptop.

1139
00:49:08,141 --> 00:49:09,775
There was a section
showing our personal funds.

1140
00:49:09,776 --> 00:49:10,809
Right.

1141
00:49:10,810 --> 00:49:13,112
I'm not sure
if you can see everyone else's too.

1142
00:49:13,614 --> 00:49:14,613
So...

1143
00:49:15,149 --> 00:49:17,950
If someone has 2,000 dollars there,

1144
00:49:18,218 --> 00:49:20,152
- You get caught right away.
- they'll get caught.

1145
00:49:22,088 --> 00:49:24,556
Wouldn't they keep
other people's funds hidden?

1146
00:49:24,791 --> 00:49:26,792
I don't think they would.

1147
00:49:32,899 --> 00:49:36,568
(Second: Michael)

1148
00:49:45,545 --> 00:49:47,312
There are eight people after me.

1149
00:49:50,551 --> 00:49:52,585
They are making me even consider this.

1150
00:49:52,586 --> 00:49:54,153
(Personal Funds Slip)

1151
00:49:54,588 --> 00:49:56,556
(Tenth: Gray)

1152
00:49:59,359 --> 00:50:00,526
This is fun.

1153
00:50:01,962 --> 00:50:05,231
This is bad for the last person.

1154
00:50:05,232 --> 00:50:07,566
The second-to-last person can see...

1155
00:50:07,567 --> 00:50:09,302
how much money is left,

1156
00:50:09,537 --> 00:50:12,537
so they get to decide
how much the last person gets.

1157
00:50:12,538 --> 00:50:13,572
I'm second to last.

1158
00:50:13,573 --> 00:50:14,708
That's me.

1159
00:50:14,709 --> 00:50:15,874
(Polar Bear, Superman)

1160
00:50:17,911 --> 00:50:21,046
(Eleventh: Polar Bear)

1161
00:50:37,097 --> 00:50:39,598
(Remaining amount: 3,000 dollars)

1162
00:50:41,101 --> 00:50:42,768
(Amount: 3,000 dollars)

1163
00:50:47,541 --> 00:50:50,676
I used a little trick
to get people to share the money...

1164
00:50:50,677 --> 00:50:52,445
instead of hogging it.

1165
00:50:52,847 --> 00:50:55,548
I remembered seeing "personal funds,"

1166
00:50:55,549 --> 00:50:58,551
and I wasn't sure
if you could see others' too.

1167
00:50:58,552 --> 00:51:00,019
I mentioned it on purpose.

1168
00:51:00,386 --> 00:51:02,754
I figured if I could plant the idea...

1169
00:51:02,755 --> 00:51:05,590
that other people could see
how much they took,

1170
00:51:06,093 --> 00:51:07,626
people would be more likely...

1171
00:51:07,627 --> 00:51:09,662
to play nice and take a reasonable amount.

1172
00:51:09,763 --> 00:51:11,563
I wasn't sure...

1173
00:51:11,564 --> 00:51:13,331
if people could see how much I took.

1174
00:51:13,867 --> 00:51:15,334
So I played it safe...

1175
00:51:15,902 --> 00:51:17,035
and didn't take much.

1176
00:51:17,203 --> 00:51:19,838
- It's 1,000 dollars.
- I'll take 1,000 dollars.

1177
00:51:19,839 --> 00:51:22,207
- It's 2,000 dollars.
- It's 2,000 dollars.

1178
00:51:22,543 --> 00:51:24,143
I was second to last.

1179
00:51:24,144 --> 00:51:26,411
Superman was the last person to go.

1180
00:51:26,412 --> 00:51:30,149
He could've ended up with zero dollars.

1181
00:51:30,150 --> 00:51:33,485
He chose to go last, knowing the risk,

1182
00:51:33,486 --> 00:51:35,688
so I felt like I should look out
for him a little.

1183
00:51:36,289 --> 00:51:37,990
(Superman)

1184
00:51:37,991 --> 00:51:39,925
(Genie)

1185
00:51:39,926 --> 00:51:42,694
There were 10,000 dollars left in my turn.

1186
00:51:42,829 --> 00:51:45,097
I didn't expect that much to be left.

1187
00:51:45,098 --> 00:51:47,065
There were four people left, including me.

1188
00:51:47,066 --> 00:51:49,868
If we split it evenly,
it would be 3, 3, 2, 2.

1189
00:51:49,869 --> 00:51:52,471
I didn't think I needed to take two.

1190
00:51:53,206 --> 00:51:54,573
Now I regret it.

1191
00:51:54,874 --> 00:51:56,341
I should've taken 4,000 dollars.

1192
00:51:58,578 --> 00:52:00,178
(Amount: 3,000 dollars, Genie, Superman)

1193
00:52:00,179 --> 00:52:02,314
(Amount: 2,000 dollars,
Polar Bear, Gray, Ted, Michael)

1194
00:52:02,315 --> 00:52:04,883
(Amount: 1,000 dollars, Go Ae Shin, Sugar,
Nangja, Benjamin, Dark Knight, Hippo)

1195
00:52:05,553 --> 00:52:08,988
The funds have been distributed
to all residents.

1196
00:52:09,690 --> 00:52:10,956
Now,

1197
00:52:11,191 --> 00:52:15,127
you'll be collecting a community fund
to pay for your shared expenses.

1198
00:52:15,962 --> 00:52:16,996
Collect a community fund?

1199
00:52:17,263 --> 00:52:18,563
It says "collect."

1200
00:52:18,832 --> 00:52:21,100
- It'll be collected from the residents.
- Sounds like it.

1201
00:52:21,401 --> 00:52:23,302
It's like paying taxes.

1202
00:52:23,303 --> 00:52:24,403
We have to pay taxes!

1203
00:52:24,805 --> 00:52:26,806
Right! Nothing is free.

1204
00:52:27,440 --> 00:52:31,476
The community fund is shared money
that can be used at the Community Mart...

1205
00:52:31,477 --> 00:52:32,878
and for activities that generate income.

1206
00:52:33,179 --> 00:52:35,313
All meals have to be purchased...

1207
00:52:35,615 --> 00:52:37,916
using the community fund.

1208
00:52:37,917 --> 00:52:41,956
(The community fund)

1209
00:52:41,957 --> 00:52:43,589
The community fund is collected
once a day.

1210
00:52:44,024 --> 00:52:45,692
The leader collects it...

1211
00:52:45,693 --> 00:52:48,595
from every resident
after the daytime schedule ends.

1212
00:52:49,196 --> 00:52:51,430
The leader can choose the collection rate,

1213
00:52:51,532 --> 00:52:54,867
starting at 10 percent,
in 10-percent increments.

1214
00:52:56,704 --> 00:52:58,971
If it's starting at ten percent...

1215
00:52:58,972 --> 00:53:00,806
It should be at least ten percent.

1216
00:53:00,875 --> 00:53:02,874
It's a fair society.

1217
00:53:03,176 --> 00:53:05,744
The leader has complete authority
over the collection rate...

1218
00:53:05,745 --> 00:53:07,446
the use of the community fund.

1219
00:53:07,880 --> 00:53:10,582
And 1,000 dollars
from the collected funds...

1220
00:53:10,750 --> 00:53:13,485
goes into the leader's personal funds.

1221
00:53:15,388 --> 00:53:17,389
The leader has more power than I thought.

1222
00:53:18,892 --> 00:53:21,160
The community fund is for everyone.

1223
00:53:21,661 --> 00:53:24,897
Any community funds left unused
by the end of the stay...

1224
00:53:24,898 --> 00:53:27,165
will be used for everyone.

1225
00:53:31,071 --> 00:53:33,372
If it's at least ten percent,

1226
00:53:33,573 --> 00:53:35,407
it can be 100 percent too.

1227
00:53:35,408 --> 00:53:36,341
Right.

1228
00:53:37,511 --> 00:53:39,211
That's possible.

1229
00:53:39,212 --> 00:53:40,412
That's practically robbery.

1230
00:53:41,281 --> 00:53:43,515
I even pay a lot of taxes in real life.

1231
00:53:44,684 --> 00:53:46,385
It's basically communism.

1232
00:53:48,688 --> 00:53:51,423
We're building a country together.

1233
00:53:51,891 --> 00:53:53,391
I thought it was really fun.

1234
00:53:53,392 --> 00:53:55,994
We were creating a small society
of our own.

1235
00:53:55,995 --> 00:53:57,095
A community.

1236
00:53:57,263 --> 00:54:00,532
And through the tax system, you get to see
big government or small government.

1237
00:54:01,000 --> 00:54:02,934
I thought that was an interesting setup.

1238
00:54:03,603 --> 00:54:06,405
It's called a microcosm in English.

1239
00:54:06,406 --> 00:54:09,074
A small-scale model...

1240
00:54:09,075 --> 00:54:11,877
of the larger social mechanisms
in modern society.

1241
00:54:11,878 --> 00:54:14,746
Everyone is taxed
at the same percentage rate.

1242
00:54:14,747 --> 00:54:16,515
A certain portion...

1243
00:54:16,516 --> 00:54:19,918
of each person's income is taken
as taxes...

1244
00:54:19,919 --> 00:54:22,487
and used for the public good.

1245
00:54:22,488 --> 00:54:25,624
I immediately thought
it was basically a tax system.

1246
00:54:27,560 --> 00:54:30,162
The community fund is whatever's left
after 1,000 dollars is taken out.

1247
00:54:30,163 --> 00:54:31,062
Right.

1248
00:54:31,632 --> 00:54:34,833
The leader has benefits.

1249
00:54:35,468 --> 00:54:36,735
A 1,000-dollar incentive.

1250
00:54:36,937 --> 00:54:37,903
My money.

1251
00:54:39,806 --> 00:54:41,106
I know.

1252
00:54:41,475 --> 00:54:43,375
If that's the case,

1253
00:54:43,710 --> 00:54:45,611
you should be on the leader's good side.

1254
00:54:46,680 --> 00:54:49,548
It sounded good,
but I was also a little uncomfortable.

1255
00:54:49,549 --> 00:54:51,850
I did want to get that money.

1256
00:54:51,851 --> 00:54:54,219
It would've been nice...

1257
00:54:54,554 --> 00:54:57,456
if I could've gotten it secretly,
but it was so out in the open.

1258
00:54:57,592 --> 00:55:00,526
So I felt a little awkward about it.

1259
00:55:00,827 --> 00:55:02,361
The leader gets...

1260
00:55:02,462 --> 00:55:04,696
to set the tax rate however they want,

1261
00:55:04,697 --> 00:55:06,798
and on top of that,
they take 1,000 dollars for themselves.

1262
00:55:06,799 --> 00:55:08,033
That seemed pretty unfair.

1263
00:55:08,034 --> 00:55:11,503
I thought this could cause
some problems later.

1264
00:55:11,528 --> 00:55:14,630
How much are you going to convince people
when you collect the tax?

1265
00:55:14,775 --> 00:55:16,576
You need to collect 60 percent,

1266
00:55:16,577 --> 00:55:18,210
and they might call you a tyrant
and kick you out.

1267
00:55:18,211 --> 00:55:20,813
And how effectively are you going
to use the money...

1268
00:55:20,814 --> 00:55:22,681
so people don't think you're wasteful?

1269
00:55:22,682 --> 00:55:24,183
How are you going to spend it?

1270
00:55:24,184 --> 00:55:26,351
Are you going to spend generously
or be more careful with it?

1271
00:55:26,352 --> 00:55:28,053
That's where things could get messy.

1272
00:55:29,790 --> 00:55:32,958
Let's choose what to eat today first.

1273
00:55:33,692 --> 00:55:36,695
We have to spend
at least 500 dollars to eat.

1274
00:55:36,696 --> 00:55:39,131
Pork belly or fried chicken costs
1,000 dollars.

1275
00:55:39,132 --> 00:55:41,333
- I want to eat pork belly.
- Ingredients are 500 dollars.

1276
00:55:41,701 --> 00:55:44,169
If you only want to spend 200 dollars,
you get eggs and potatoes.

1277
00:55:44,604 --> 00:55:46,238
That's how it goes.

1278
00:55:46,239 --> 00:55:48,206
Even if we do ten percent,

1279
00:55:48,207 --> 00:55:50,075
we can eat better than this.

1280
00:55:50,076 --> 00:55:51,043
You're right.

1281
00:55:51,311 --> 00:55:54,680
The leader takes 1,000 dollars
no matter what.

1282
00:55:54,881 --> 00:55:56,682
So if we only pay ten percent,

1283
00:55:57,650 --> 00:55:59,684
that's not enough for food.

1284
00:56:00,020 --> 00:56:02,354
We should pay at least 20 to 30 percent.

1285
00:56:02,922 --> 00:56:05,057
Remember, the leader gets 1,000 dollars.

1286
00:56:05,692 --> 00:56:09,161
Since everyone has a similar amount today,

1287
00:56:09,162 --> 00:56:12,798
we don't need to go with the minimum.

1288
00:56:12,799 --> 00:56:14,600
If we do 30 percent...

1289
00:56:15,401 --> 00:56:16,501
That'd be 6,000 dollars.

1290
00:56:16,502 --> 00:56:17,502
- It's 6,000 dollars?
- Yes.

1291
00:56:17,503 --> 00:56:19,270
(At a 30-percent tax rate,
Community fund: 5,000 dollars)

1292
00:56:20,040 --> 00:56:21,205
Let's choose what to eat...

1293
00:56:21,206 --> 00:56:22,342
for dinner today.

1294
00:56:22,343 --> 00:56:23,842
I recommend pork belly.

1295
00:56:23,843 --> 00:56:25,477
- Me too.
- I vote for pork belly too.

1296
00:56:25,478 --> 00:56:27,646
- Sounds good.
- Who wants pork belly?

1297
00:56:27,647 --> 00:56:28,613
Me.

1298
00:56:29,616 --> 00:56:31,016
Most of us want pork belly.

1299
00:56:32,252 --> 00:56:33,953
You guys want to throw a party.

1300
00:56:34,721 --> 00:56:35,653
How about ramyeon?

1301
00:56:36,289 --> 00:56:38,657
Ramyeon doesn't sound bad.

1302
00:56:38,859 --> 00:56:39,792
Ramyeon sounds good.

1303
00:56:40,059 --> 00:56:40,960
Packaged ramyeon?

1304
00:56:41,495 --> 00:56:42,494
Ramyeon sounds good.

1305
00:56:42,495 --> 00:56:43,929
(Pork belly and soju: 1,000 dollars,
12 packaged ramyeon: 300 dollars)

1306
00:56:44,164 --> 00:56:46,298
It's only the first day,
so let's treat ourselves.

1307
00:56:47,200 --> 00:56:49,268
Then 1,000 dollars.

1308
00:56:50,203 --> 00:56:53,739
I didn't want to eat pork belly
and fried chicken on the first day.

1309
00:56:54,174 --> 00:56:56,107
I wanted to eat eggs and potatoes.

1310
00:56:56,608 --> 00:56:58,076
But then I thought...

1311
00:56:58,077 --> 00:57:00,879
that might make them think
I'm an Eco-feminist.

1312
00:57:01,414 --> 00:57:02,381
Pretty sure.

1313
00:57:02,581 --> 00:57:05,683
They might even say I was vegan,
so I stayed quiet.

1314
00:57:06,953 --> 00:57:08,587
Actually, I didn't agree.

1315
00:57:09,027 --> 00:57:12,096
I could survive a week without food.

1316
00:57:12,292 --> 00:57:14,259
I was trained to be like that,

1317
00:57:14,394 --> 00:57:16,127
so I have a different mindset from people.

1318
00:57:17,864 --> 00:57:19,464
The Americano's a little pricey.

1319
00:57:19,465 --> 00:57:20,766
Right.

1320
00:57:21,067 --> 00:57:22,300
The Americano is expensive.

1321
00:57:22,301 --> 00:57:24,036
I drink it all day.

1322
00:57:24,270 --> 00:57:26,738
There are many other options.

1323
00:57:27,440 --> 00:57:28,373
You should drink tea.

1324
00:57:28,474 --> 00:57:29,941
Do that instead.

1325
00:57:31,411 --> 00:57:32,444
Thank you.

1326
00:57:32,445 --> 00:57:35,547
People who drink iced Americanos
every morning might see it differently...

1327
00:57:35,548 --> 00:57:37,283
from people who don't.

1328
00:57:37,284 --> 00:57:38,617
Let me ask everyone.

1329
00:57:38,618 --> 00:57:41,687
I need my Americano in the morning.

1330
00:57:41,988 --> 00:57:43,154
Don't be shy.

1331
00:57:43,155 --> 00:57:45,323
("I drink Americano," nine people)

1332
00:57:45,324 --> 00:57:48,960
("I don't drink Americano," three people)

1333
00:57:49,330 --> 00:57:53,432
I'll just set aside 1,200 dollars
for this.

1334
00:57:53,433 --> 00:57:55,868
(Even budgeting for the people
who don't drink Americano)

1335
00:57:56,803 --> 00:57:58,103
You don't eat but drink this.

1336
00:57:59,505 --> 00:58:01,339
You guys really love your coffee.

1337
00:58:01,874 --> 00:58:04,209
That's already 3,000 dollars.

1338
00:58:04,210 --> 00:58:06,077
With Americanos, it's 3,000 dollars.

1339
00:58:06,078 --> 00:58:08,146
Americanos are expensive.

1340
00:58:08,147 --> 00:58:10,481
Let's drink it
because it's just the first day.

1341
00:58:11,417 --> 00:58:12,250
All right.

1342
00:58:13,586 --> 00:58:16,421
Where did these people come from?

1343
00:58:16,923 --> 00:58:19,825
After getting a good look at everyone,

1344
00:58:20,127 --> 00:58:23,262
they didn't seem to have
that hungry, survival mentality.

1345
00:58:23,263 --> 00:58:25,931
Most people here seem...

1346
00:58:25,932 --> 00:58:27,599
pretty financially comfortable.

1347
00:58:28,068 --> 00:58:30,502
What do they do? Where am I?

1348
00:58:31,604 --> 00:58:34,406
Shall we get ingredients
to see what they're like?

1349
00:58:35,141 --> 00:58:36,308
Let's do 30 percent, then.

1350
00:58:36,509 --> 00:58:37,576
I'll go put that.

1351
00:58:40,213 --> 00:58:44,516
(Confirmed to collect 30 percent
for the community fund)

1352
00:58:44,851 --> 00:58:46,784
Thirty percent...

1353
00:58:47,219 --> 00:58:51,022
of everyone's personal funds was collected
for the community fund,

1354
00:58:51,457 --> 00:58:55,860
and 1,000 dollars from the fund
will go to the leader.

1355
00:58:56,396 --> 00:59:01,000
(Personal Funds Status)

1356
00:59:01,001 --> 00:59:03,802
(Community fund: 5,000 dollars)

1357
00:59:03,803 --> 00:59:05,537
- Okay.
- Good work.

1358
00:59:05,538 --> 00:59:06,905
Nice work.

1359
00:59:09,009 --> 00:59:12,677
I promised that I wouldn't let
people go hungry...

1360
00:59:12,678 --> 00:59:14,246
when I ran for the leader,

1361
00:59:14,247 --> 00:59:16,682
so I wanted everyone...

1362
00:59:16,683 --> 00:59:18,250
to be able to enjoy plenty of food.

1363
00:59:18,651 --> 00:59:21,119
I figured nobody would have
too much of a problem with it.

1364
00:59:21,354 --> 00:59:23,922
Since I had 3,000 dollars,

1365
00:59:24,124 --> 00:59:27,325
that extra 10 percent made
a big difference.

1366
00:59:27,326 --> 00:59:30,428
But a lot of people took
1,000 dollars today.

1367
00:59:30,730 --> 00:59:33,532
Even if they saved 100 dollars,

1368
00:59:33,533 --> 00:59:35,934
they could only afford a carton of milk.

1369
00:59:36,336 --> 00:59:38,970
So I didn't think anyone would be
too upset.

1370
00:59:39,272 --> 00:59:42,642
We ended up spending money like crazy.
We made ridiculous purchases.

1371
00:59:42,643 --> 00:59:44,943
If I were the leader,

1372
00:59:44,944 --> 00:59:47,512
I'd try to get the majority
on board first...

1373
00:59:47,513 --> 00:59:49,715
and give up on the people
I couldn't convince.

1374
00:59:49,983 --> 00:59:52,284
We skipped a lot of the steps today.

1375
00:59:52,585 --> 00:59:55,754
Everyone got a generous share...

1376
00:59:55,755 --> 00:59:57,422
of the public resources.

1377
00:59:57,557 --> 01:00:00,925
We were all spending
like there was no tomorrow...

1378
01:00:00,926 --> 01:00:02,794
and throwing one last party
before going bankrupt.

1379
01:00:03,028 --> 01:00:04,462
Some people still have plenty of money,

1380
01:00:04,463 --> 01:00:06,864
while others might already be
completely broke.

1381
01:00:06,865 --> 01:00:10,067
We're already starting
from different places now.

1382
01:00:10,068 --> 01:00:13,438
Tomorrow's leader has
to do things differently from today.

1383
01:00:13,939 --> 01:00:16,641
Shall we order pork belly first?

1384
01:00:18,978 --> 01:00:21,546
(Welcome. This is the Community Mart.)

1385
01:00:21,937 --> 01:00:24,442
Welcome. This is the Community Mart.

1386
01:00:25,618 --> 01:00:27,586
We'd like to order 3kg of pork belly...

1387
01:00:27,587 --> 01:00:30,155
and ingredients.

1388
01:00:30,923 --> 01:00:32,090
(Total: 1,500 dollars)

1389
01:00:32,091 --> 01:00:34,926
Please check your total. Would you like
to proceed with the purchase?

1390
01:00:34,927 --> 01:00:36,261
Yes. I'll buy it.

1391
01:00:37,230 --> 01:00:38,797
Your purchase has been completed.

1392
01:00:39,066 --> 01:00:40,332
It'll be deducted from the community fund.

1393
01:00:40,333 --> 01:00:43,368
You'll be notified
once your order is delivered.

1394
01:00:44,971 --> 01:00:46,471
Where's the smoking area?

1395
01:00:46,472 --> 01:00:47,439
I'll show you the way.

1396
01:00:48,441 --> 01:00:49,608
What are you up to?

1397
01:00:49,609 --> 01:00:51,143
- I'm going to have a smoke.
- A smoke.

1398
01:00:51,477 --> 01:00:53,578
- Can I come with you?
- Of course.

1399
01:00:56,582 --> 01:00:58,916
It's different from other survival shows.

1400
01:00:58,917 --> 01:01:01,887
It feels like we're running a country.

1401
01:01:01,888 --> 01:01:04,991
It's about how we handle the money...

1402
01:01:04,992 --> 01:01:06,791
and how the leader gathers
everyone's opinions.

1403
01:01:07,227 --> 01:01:10,428
When we were picking the leader,

1404
01:01:11,365 --> 01:01:13,532
didn't all the women vote for women?

1405
01:01:14,100 --> 01:01:15,233
Didn't it seem like it?

1406
01:01:20,740 --> 01:01:24,342
Since we have the same number
of men and women,

1407
01:01:24,878 --> 01:01:27,979
I could see this turning...

1408
01:01:28,415 --> 01:01:29,881
into a gender battle.

1409
01:01:30,316 --> 01:01:31,182
Right.

1410
01:01:32,885 --> 01:01:34,352
It was funny.

1411
01:01:34,520 --> 01:01:35,887
He doesn't even smoke.

1412
01:01:35,888 --> 01:01:38,857
He followed us and suddenly said...

1413
01:01:38,858 --> 01:01:40,458
that he felt like...

1414
01:01:40,560 --> 01:01:42,628
all the women voted for the leader.

1415
01:01:42,795 --> 01:01:46,030
I feel like he was kind of trying
to stir up some gender conflict.

1416
01:01:46,666 --> 01:01:50,802
It seemed like he was trying
to divide the men and women.

1417
01:01:50,803 --> 01:01:53,238
I wondered if he said that on purpose...

1418
01:01:53,239 --> 01:01:55,641
to get us to see it that way.

1419
01:01:56,042 --> 01:01:57,709
I was a little suspicious.

1420
01:01:58,278 --> 01:02:00,178
I don't know. I'll keep an eye on him.

1421
01:02:02,148 --> 01:02:04,683
Your order has arrived.

1422
01:02:04,684 --> 01:02:05,550
Finally!

1423
01:02:05,952 --> 01:02:06,818
Where is it?

1424
01:02:08,754 --> 01:02:09,887
That's a lot of stuff.

1425
01:02:10,356 --> 01:02:12,991
(Their order has arrived.)

1426
01:02:12,992 --> 01:02:14,961
It's delivered like this.

1427
01:02:14,962 --> 01:02:17,595
They give us raw rice,

1428
01:02:17,596 --> 01:02:18,630
so we have to cook it ourselves.

1429
01:02:18,631 --> 01:02:19,531
Is there soju too?

1430
01:02:19,532 --> 01:02:20,632
Oh, there's cookware.

1431
01:02:21,434 --> 01:02:22,734
- Should we put it like this?
- This way.

1432
01:02:22,735 --> 01:02:24,436
- Can we open it?
- Like this?

1433
01:02:27,306 --> 01:02:29,742
I'll soak the rice.

1434
01:02:29,743 --> 01:02:31,442
I saw a rice cooker earlier.

1435
01:02:31,443 --> 01:02:33,911
I was hoping we'd get ready-made rice.

1436
01:02:34,314 --> 01:02:35,580
Have you used a knife much?

1437
01:02:35,581 --> 01:02:37,816
Yes. I make pasta.

1438
01:02:38,718 --> 01:02:40,886
I just make it like ramyeon.

1439
01:02:52,832 --> 01:02:54,032
Lady.

1440
01:02:54,033 --> 01:02:55,600
- Lady.
- Yes?

1441
01:02:56,035 --> 01:02:57,736
You're going to cut yourself.

1442
01:02:58,271 --> 01:02:59,872
When you cut that...

1443
01:02:59,873 --> 01:03:02,507
I usually make something simple.

1444
01:03:02,743 --> 01:03:04,810
I'm sure the onions at home...

1445
01:03:04,811 --> 01:03:06,611
were never like that.

1446
01:03:06,612 --> 01:03:08,780
Maybe I didn't notice
when I cut one onion.

1447
01:03:08,781 --> 01:03:10,248
I just cut an onion like this.

1448
01:03:10,249 --> 01:03:13,618
I realized I'd never cut
green onions before.

1449
01:03:13,619 --> 01:03:15,887
Just cut it like this.

1450
01:03:15,888 --> 01:03:17,255
If someone complains, just say it was me.

1451
01:03:17,424 --> 01:03:20,859
Nangja was kind of babying me...

1452
01:03:20,860 --> 01:03:23,096
in a loving way.

1453
01:03:23,097 --> 01:03:26,264
She seemed really sweet.

1454
01:03:26,399 --> 01:03:28,100
She had no idea...

1455
01:03:28,101 --> 01:03:30,268
how to hold a knife...

1456
01:03:30,269 --> 01:03:31,670
or even how to prep vegetables.

1457
01:03:31,671 --> 01:03:34,706
It's clear that she hasn't really lived
on her own yet...

1458
01:03:34,707 --> 01:03:37,743
or done much cooking.

1459
01:03:37,744 --> 01:03:39,644
She and I are probably very different.

1460
01:03:39,645 --> 01:03:41,113
(Nangja, Genie)

1461
01:03:42,049 --> 01:03:43,582
Can I help with anything else?

1462
01:03:43,983 --> 01:03:46,284
- We just have to peel one more.
- Okay.

1463
01:03:49,655 --> 01:03:50,755
Do you want to smoke?

1464
01:03:51,057 --> 01:03:52,324
- Shall we?
- Yes.

1465
01:03:53,827 --> 01:03:57,029
Do you think nine days will fly by?

1466
01:03:57,030 --> 01:03:58,263
- No, I don't.
- Right.

1467
01:03:58,264 --> 01:04:01,266
Do you start to see what people are
really like after a few days?

1468
01:04:01,534 --> 01:04:04,503
- You don't know yet.
- But they can't hide it, right?

1469
01:04:04,603 --> 01:04:05,536
They can't.

1470
01:04:05,806 --> 01:04:08,773
It's hard to hide what you really believe.

1471
01:04:08,774 --> 01:04:11,209
Sooner or later,
a couple of people will start losing it.

1472
01:04:11,744 --> 01:04:13,245
It's bound to happen.

1473
01:04:13,246 --> 01:04:14,680
I wonder
if people will start teaming up...

1474
01:04:14,681 --> 01:04:17,316
based on their political views as we go.

1475
01:04:17,317 --> 01:04:18,349
Yes.

1476
01:04:27,226 --> 01:04:31,229
White rice!
Cuckoo is starting the cooking process.

1477
01:04:31,864 --> 01:04:32,831
White rice cooking?

1478
01:04:33,432 --> 01:04:35,300
Is it because the rice cooker said that?

1479
01:04:35,301 --> 01:04:37,235
- Sorry?
- White rice cooking.

1480
01:04:37,703 --> 01:04:38,670
Is that hard?

1481
01:04:38,871 --> 01:04:41,539
- No. I got it.
- You did?

1482
01:04:42,041 --> 01:04:44,342
This is what Benjamin should be like.

1483
01:04:44,610 --> 01:04:47,278
He needs to come across as a foreigner.

1484
01:04:47,680 --> 01:04:49,914
I need everyone else to believe that.

1485
01:04:51,283 --> 01:04:53,885
My Korean is good.

1486
01:04:54,586 --> 01:04:56,421
I'm not super fluent
in either Korean or English.

1487
01:04:56,856 --> 01:04:58,224
(Glancing)

1488
01:04:58,225 --> 01:05:01,326
You seem like someone
I can find at a startup.

1489
01:05:02,027 --> 01:05:03,461
I quit a startup.

1490
01:05:03,996 --> 01:05:05,229
You're like me.

1491
01:05:05,937 --> 01:05:07,871
- I quit my job exactly five months ago.
- On the same page!

1492
01:05:08,267 --> 01:05:09,867
Five months?

1493
01:05:09,868 --> 01:05:11,936
Because I wanted to try something new.

1494
01:05:12,632 --> 01:05:15,406
(Michael continues
the English conversation.)

1495
01:05:16,075 --> 01:05:17,875
Seriously. Michael!

1496
01:05:18,244 --> 01:05:19,611
I hope he goes home.

1497
01:05:20,246 --> 01:05:22,347
We have similar characters.

1498
01:05:23,581 --> 01:05:26,618
(Keep talking in English)

1499
01:05:26,619 --> 01:05:28,653
We were at the point
where we had to upscale.

1500
01:05:28,988 --> 01:05:31,389
If I keep talking to him,
my cover will be blown.

1501
01:05:31,390 --> 01:05:33,358
I'm gonna keep at it for as long as I can.

1502
01:05:33,992 --> 01:05:35,859
He might figure me out.

1503
01:05:35,860 --> 01:05:37,562
Then I'll change my strategy.

1504
01:05:37,563 --> 01:05:40,031
If he doesn't, I'll just keep going.

1505
01:05:40,866 --> 01:05:41,766
Michael.

1506
01:05:42,668 --> 01:05:44,435
To be honest,
I've lived in Korea my whole life.

1507
01:05:46,972 --> 01:05:49,073
Benjamin is such a weirdo.

1508
01:05:50,877 --> 01:05:51,843
I'd better wash the spoons.

1509
01:05:53,747 --> 01:05:54,646
Let's eat.

1510
01:05:54,647 --> 01:05:56,014
- Let's eat.
- Let's eat.

1511
01:05:56,015 --> 01:05:57,516
It's not much, but dig in.

1512
01:05:57,884 --> 01:05:59,951
You did a good job grilling the meat.

1513
01:05:59,952 --> 01:06:01,987
It's so good.

1514
01:06:02,721 --> 01:06:03,721
It looks good.

1515
01:06:08,461 --> 01:06:09,428
It's delicious.

1516
01:06:10,429 --> 01:06:11,462
The garlic is grilled just right.

1517
01:06:13,732 --> 01:06:15,166
How do you take good care of your skin?

1518
01:06:15,167 --> 01:06:16,167
I don't take care of it.

1519
01:06:16,302 --> 01:06:18,770
My parents just look young.

1520
01:06:18,771 --> 01:06:20,604
You have a pretty easy, cushy job.

1521
01:06:20,605 --> 01:06:21,973
- A cushy job?
- Yes.

1522
01:06:23,375 --> 01:06:24,742
A cushy, comfortable job.

1523
01:06:24,743 --> 01:06:26,977
(Dark Knight and Gray)

1524
01:06:26,978 --> 01:06:28,512
- Are you a doctor?
- A doctor?

1525
01:06:29,014 --> 01:06:30,414
- A lawyer.
- A lawyer?

1526
01:06:30,716 --> 01:06:31,616
A professor?

1527
01:06:31,617 --> 01:06:33,351
- A young professor.
- Thank you.

1528
01:06:33,352 --> 01:06:34,452
I like how I come across.

1529
01:06:34,453 --> 01:06:35,486
(Guessing Gray's job)

1530
01:06:35,988 --> 01:06:39,590
They guessed I might be a lawyer
or a doctor.

1531
01:06:39,591 --> 01:06:41,392
That was ridiculous.

1532
01:06:41,827 --> 01:06:44,262
Dark Knight looks like
he's spent some time on a yacht.

1533
01:06:44,997 --> 01:06:46,031
- Surfing?
- Surfing?

1534
01:06:46,032 --> 01:06:47,532
A wakeboard instructor.

1535
01:06:47,733 --> 01:06:49,701
Is that really all I look like I'd do?

1536
01:06:50,102 --> 01:06:52,437
You don't think
I could be a doctor or a lawyer?

1537
01:06:52,905 --> 01:06:53,905
A doctor!

1538
01:06:54,874 --> 01:06:55,773
A prosecutor.

1539
01:06:57,409 --> 01:07:00,411
I'm sure there's a politician among us.

1540
01:07:01,113 --> 01:07:03,014
I really feel like
Polar Bear is a politician.

1541
01:07:03,117 --> 01:07:05,149
I thought so too.

1542
01:07:05,951 --> 01:07:07,818
- Why is that?
- She'd do a good job.

1543
01:07:08,787 --> 01:07:10,087
You seem really intellectual.

1544
01:07:10,589 --> 01:07:12,723
I know for sure that she's sharp.

1545
01:07:12,724 --> 01:07:14,483
- She seems resourceful.
- I agree.

1546
01:07:15,361 --> 01:07:18,696
I realized I'm pretty predictable.

1547
01:07:19,032 --> 01:07:22,533
Is it really obvious that I'm a politician?

1548
01:07:23,069 --> 01:07:25,403
Then it would be too predictable,

1549
01:07:25,404 --> 01:07:27,337
so that made me think she might not.

1550
01:07:27,338 --> 01:07:28,739
Hippo could be a politician.

1551
01:07:30,510 --> 01:07:31,709
That's possible.

1552
01:07:31,710 --> 01:07:33,044
Guess which party I'm with.

1553
01:07:34,380 --> 01:07:35,513
Which party am I with?

1554
01:07:35,514 --> 01:07:37,115
(A former Femidangdang activist)

1555
01:07:37,316 --> 01:07:38,316
This is fun.

1556
01:07:39,585 --> 01:07:41,519
- This is what it's like.
- I'm curious.

1557
01:07:43,122 --> 01:07:47,626
(The announcement will now begin.)

1558
01:07:47,827 --> 01:07:50,661
This is an announcement
from the Community Center.

1559
01:07:51,798 --> 01:07:54,432
Regarding the nighttime schedule
after dinner,

1560
01:07:54,634 --> 01:07:58,670
we'll provide some brief information
about the residents.

1561
01:08:00,673 --> 01:08:01,573
What could it be?

1562
01:08:02,844 --> 01:08:04,275
The residents gave...

1563
01:08:04,711 --> 01:08:07,645
the following responses to this question.

1564
01:08:12,918 --> 01:08:16,787
"All men are potential perpetrators."

1565
01:08:24,330 --> 01:08:25,830
"I agree." Two.

1566
01:08:26,498 --> 01:08:28,266
"I disagree." Ten.

1567
01:08:32,538 --> 01:08:35,340
They just dropped that topic
out of nowhere.

1568
01:08:35,741 --> 01:08:39,610
When it popped up,
everyone seemed a little rattled.

1569
01:08:39,812 --> 01:08:41,780
It was like the calm before the storm.

1570
01:08:43,249 --> 01:08:46,384
We hope it helped you understand
each other better.

1571
01:08:54,126 --> 01:08:56,461
The silence among us.

1572
01:08:56,462 --> 01:08:57,628
That awkward tension.

1573
01:09:01,560 --> 01:09:02,493
Two.

1574
01:09:03,235 --> 01:09:04,235
Ten.

1575
01:09:05,938 --> 01:09:08,907
Two people agreed to that statement?

1576
01:09:09,208 --> 01:09:13,244
I'm guessing they were women.

1577
01:09:13,413 --> 01:09:14,512
Don't you watch the news?

1578
01:09:14,513 --> 01:09:18,015
There are plenty of husbands
who abuse their wives.

1579
01:09:18,350 --> 01:09:20,851
She might have been hit by a man.

1580
01:09:20,953 --> 01:09:23,054
You don't know their full story.

1581
01:09:23,322 --> 01:09:25,423
But don't lose hope.

1582
01:09:25,625 --> 01:09:27,692
Not all men are potential threats.

1583
01:09:28,360 --> 01:09:30,396
No one here...

1584
01:09:30,397 --> 01:09:34,499
seemed that hostile toward men.

1585
01:09:34,867 --> 01:09:36,167
I was a little confused.

1586
01:09:37,770 --> 01:09:39,704
I guess this is
what we'll be debating tonight.

1587
01:09:41,406 --> 01:09:44,441
(The anonymous chat debate begins.)

1588
01:09:44,442 --> 01:09:46,244
When talking about gender...

1589
01:09:46,245 --> 01:09:49,113
They reveal their true colors
behind anonymity.

1590
01:09:49,448 --> 01:09:51,015
What are they saying?

1591
01:09:51,784 --> 01:09:52,917
Are you from overseas?

1592
01:09:53,119 --> 01:09:54,852
The debate got really heated.

1593
01:09:55,021 --> 01:09:57,489
Some people get hurt and shocked.

1594
01:09:57,890 --> 01:09:59,157
What do I do?

1595
01:10:00,719 --> 01:10:02,592
This drives me crazy.
